{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WdXeaQMALuRThueFCnnLNA","type":"tool_result","content":"nal CDN dependencies. Do not read files outside what you need - other agents share this directory. They share this machine’s ports and processes too. Prefer file:// and the engine’s headless tools. If you need a server (the AI Hub harness does), bind 127.0.0.1 on a port the OS picks (`python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` prints it) and confirm the page is your game before trusting a result. Stop only processes you started, by their PID: never pkill, killall, fuser -k, or kill whatever holds a port.\n\nPerformance: Treat the performance budget as unlimited. Add as much detail, enemies, effects, lights, and shadows as the scene needs. Out of scope (handled later): FPS targets, optimization.\n\nTesting: Test as much as you like with headless tools: scripts that play the game and check its state (the engine’s game.advance and game.debug, as in game/test.mjs), including a scripted playthrough of the whole game. Keep them behind one command that prints only failures and a one-line count, and rerun it only after changing the code it covers. Use screenshots only for what a script can’t check. Don’t use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\n\nEffort: Spend effort however you see fit.\n\n## Goal\n\nBuild an original game inspired by the source.\n\nScope\n\nCreate a feature-complete demo. Capture what makes the source distinctive (its world, characters, themes and tone) and turn it into the mechanics, pacing and UX of a great game. The demo must be long enough to show ALL features and gameplay mechanics so a player can clearly understand how the game plays and feels, and what they can and cannot do. Treat the demo as a complete, polished vertical slice. It should contain all the infrastructure, systems, and polish needed so the remainder of the game could later be built out with normal designer tools.\n\nInclude a dev mode, reachable from the pause menu, with the standard tools for testing the game quickly. Balance is key, so expose the most important gameplay variables as live sliders, with a reset to defaults.\n\nAttract Mode: After 10 seconds without input on the title/menu screen, start a looping AI-played demo or scripted replay (arcade/kiosk style); any input exits it. The AI (or replay) can be simple and may cheat freely (infinite health, ammo, abilities, no clip, etc). Its only job is to show as much of the demo as possible, the attract mode is not meant to balance the game. \n\nMultiplayer: Follow https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub for multiplayer integration into ai-hub as well as for the game on its own.\n\nNarrative tone: Use a writing style similar to the source’s.\n\n## UI & UX: 10-Foot Controller-First Philosophy\nDesign the game around a PS5 DualSense (or any pad the browser reports in its standard mapping) played on a TV at 10 feet. With keyboard and mouse it's the same game on the same screens, not a separate desktop layout, but they should feel native, not like stand-ins for controller buttons. The keyboard uses the keys PC players expect for the genre. The mouse aims, looks, and clicks menu items and things in the world (hover moves the focus) wherever the genre uses it. On-screen prompts follow the input used last: PlayStation glyphs for the controller (with a Settings option to show Xbox glyphs, A B X Y, instead), keys and mouse buttons for keyboard and mouse.\n\nDo not use overly dense, web-style data screens.\n\nTouch-only devices: no touch controls; show \"Controller or keyboard required\" and the settings button. Let the attract mode run.\n\n## Done\nNo TODOs, placeholders, or stubbed features. The demo is complete.\n\n## Source Information\nCRITICAL: Put Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback in your chat reply and an in-game About/Credits screen. Write \"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm.\n- Date: 2026-11-03 18:46:51\n- Source: the work it's based on\n- Plan: the file name of the plan you were given to build from, or none\n- Genre and Perspective: genre and camera perspective used\n- Tech: stack, libraries/engines, with versions if applicable\n- Art Direction: style used\n- Animation: system used\n- Audio: music, sound effects and voice used\n- Online: network features used, or none\n- Testing: testing parameters allowed\n- Research: research done, or none\n- Environment: Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- Model: Anthropic, claude-sonnet-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- Usage: the estimated API cost in USD, total number of tokens in K or M (provide break down if available) and session time, only if estimates are available.\n\nExit interview: before you finish, also save these answers as exit-interview.json in this folder's root, beside the game's .html: one JSON object in exactly the shape below, every value filled in (\"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm). Tools read it (the game's run record and the AI Hub catalogue), so write plain text, and check that the file parses as JSON.\n{\n  \"sourceInformation\": {\"date\": \"\", \"source\": \"\", \"plan\": \"\", \"genre\": \"\", \"perspective\": \"\", \"tech\": \"\", \"artDirection\": \"\", \"animation\": \"\", \"audio\": \"\", \"online\": \"\", \"testing\": \"\", \"research\": \"\", \"environment\": \"\", \"model\": \"\", \"usage\": \"\"},\n  \"testingFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Testing Feedback question, in order\"],\n  \"engineFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Engine Feedback question, in order\"],\n  \"promptFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Prompt Feedback question, in order\"],\n  \"aiHub\": {\n    \"name\": \"the game's title, as on its title screen\",\n    \"tagline\": \"a few words to go under the title\",\n    \"description\": \"one or two sentences for the catalogue\",\n    \"type\": \"Game, Tech demo or Tool\",\n    \"genre\": \"one of Action, Adventure, Fighting, Flight, Horror, Kids & Learning, Life Sim, Platformer, Puzzle, Racing, RPG, Shooter, Simulation, Sports, Stealth, Strategy\",\n    \"source\": \"the work it's based on, e.g. Moby-Dick\",\n    \"series\": \"its franchise, e.g. Star Fox, or empty\",\n    \"players\": \"how many can play: 1, 1–2 or 1–4\",\n    \"controls\": \"the controls, in one line\",\n    \"tags\": [\"3 to 6 lowercase tags, e.g. 2d, 3d, arcade, story, co-op, versus, first-person, third-person, top-down, side-scrolling, pixel art, low-poly, retro, sci-fi, fantasy, comedy, vehicles, boss fights, exploration\"]\n  }\n}\n\nTesting Feedback\n\n- How many issues did testing catch?\n- How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?\n- What was the most helpful test you ran?\n- What was the least helpful test you ran? \n\nEngine Feedback\n\n- What did you like most about the engine?\n- Did you get stuck or guess wrong?\n- What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?\n- What was the hardest part about using the engine?\n- What did you change in the engine, and why? (none if nothing)\n- If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?\n\nPrompt Feedback \n\n- What did you like most about this prompt?\n- What was confusing?\n- What was the hardest thing to do?\n- What would you change in this prompt?\n\n  8765 TASK.md\n162820 PLAN.md\n171585 total\n# SNIPEOUT HD\n\n> *Anti-gravity. Anti-everyone.*\n\nAn original anti-gravity combat racer inspired by **Wipeout HD**, built on the my-3D2dge engine (the handoff package\nplus its real-3D add-on). This file is the build plan. **TASK.md** is the brief and **PLAN.md** is how to build it. The\nbuilders may be other AI models working without the planner. Every number here is a starting value, unless this file\nsays a value was checked (the track layouts and jump flights were checked with a script during planning: §4.3, App. C).\n\n**Title.** TASK.md asks for a playful riff on the source's name, picked by a real random number generator. Fifty riffs\nwere brainstormed (none reuses \"Wipeout\" itself). One was drawn with Python's `secrets.randbelow(50)`, which reads\nOS entropy, and came out as entry 19 of 50: **Snipeout HD**. The title doubles as the league's slang, kept in the\nfiction: to be *sniped out* is to lose a race to a weapon in its last few hundred metres. The combat mode is called\nSNIPEOUT.\n\n**Names on disk (exact):**\n\n| What | Path (relative to this folder, the one holding TASK.md) |\n| --- | --- |\n| Dev folder (all source, tests, tools) | `SnipeoutHD-WipeoutHD-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174728/` |\n| The deliverable, one HTML file | `SnipeoutHD-WipeoutHD-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174728.html` |\n| Exit interview | `exit-interview.json` (beside the HTML) |\n\nGameTitle = `SnipeoutHD` (the title with spaces removed). SOURCE = `WipeoutHD`.\n\n---\n\n## 0. How to use this plan\n\n1. Read §1 (the game on one page), then §2 (architecture), then the system you are building in §3. The content you\n   need is in §4.\n2. Build in the order in §5. Each milestone has exit tests. Do not start polish (M12) before every feature in §8 exists.\n3. A system must be complete before it is pretty. The brief forbids TODOs, placeholders and stubs in the delivered\n   demo. A small but complete feature beats a large unfinished one. If you must cut, cut **quantity** (props, lines of\n   dialogue, number of songs), never a **feature** listed in §8.\n4. The rules from TASK.md that bind every step:\n   - Keep all files in the dev folder.\n   - Read nothing outside it, except the pinned downloads in §2.1.\n   - Never touch other agents' processes or ports. A server binds `127.0.0.1` on port 0 and is stopped by its own PID.\n   - Testing runs behind one command (§7).\n   - No subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\n5. **/tmp on this machine is a shared, nearly full tmpfs** (97–99 % used while planning). Point every tool at a temp\n   folder inside the dev folder: `TMPDIR=$PWD/.tmp` for Node, Playwright and Chromium. Put downloads in the dev folder\n   (`vendor/`, `engine/`), never in /tmp.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The game on one page\n\n**Genre:** anti-gravity combat racing (arcade), with a survival score-attack mode (OVERCLOCK) and a combat-points mode\n(SNIPEOUT).\n**Perspective:** third-person chase camera behind the craft. This is the default. △ / C cycles it through a near\nchase, a far chase and a cockpit (nose) view. A look-back button gives a rear view. Trackside \"TV\" cameras are used for\nthe race intros, replays, spectating after elimination, the podium and the attract mode. This is the source's own\ncamera set, and the right one for a racer at 300–800 km/h.\n\n**What makes Wipeout HD distinctive, and where it goes in Snipeout HD:**\n\n| Source trait | Snipeout HD mechanic / UX |\n| --- | --- |\n| Hover craft glued to roller-coaster tracks: banks, crests, drops, jumps, tunnels | Track-space hover physics on a banked 3D ribbon, with gap and drop jumps, tunnels and overpasses (§3.1, §3.2) |\n| Airbrakes as the core skill | Analog left/right airbrakes (L2/R2, Q/E) for tighter turns and slides, and both at once to brake |\n| Pitch control | Nose-down for speed and short jumps, nose-up for crests and long glides |\n| Side-shift and barrel roll | Side-shift on the ground (L1/R1, Z/X). A barrel roll in the air gives a boost but costs shield |\n| Speed pads and weapon pads | Blue chevron pads boost. Amber pads give a random weapon, weighted by position |\n| Absorb | Turn the held weapon into shield energy instead of firing it |\n| Shield energy, pit lane, elimination | Energy is lost to walls, weapons and rolls. A green pit strip recharges it. At 0 the craft is eliminated |\n| Ten weapons | SWARM, SEEKER, MINEFIELD, CANNON, RAILSHOT, TREMOR, BULWARK, AUTOPILOT, NITRO, SIPHON (§3.4) |\n| Speed classes | SPARK → FLARE → STORM → SINGULARITY, each faster (§4.2) |\n| Corporate teams, sleek liveries, graphic-design branding, deadpan announcer | Six original teams (§4.1), fictional sponsors, the Authority's race control, a gibberish-voiced announcer that names every pickup |\n| Zone mode (survive rising speed in an abstract skin) | OVERCLOCK mode with the neon-grid skin (§3.3) |\n| Eliminator | SNIPEOUT mode (points for hits and eliminations, respawns) |\n| Electronic soundtrack | Six original synthesized songs that build in layers (§3.10) |\n| HD presentation: speed, bloom, 60 fps sheen | World3D's bloom, FOV kick, speed lines, exhaust trails, pixel-perfect HUD |\n\n**Modes (all playable in the demo):** QUICK RACE, SEASON (the campaign, with story), TIME TRIAL (ghost), HOT LAP,\nSNIPEOUT, OVERCLOCK, ACADEMY (the tutorial), VERSUS (2-player split screen), REPLAY (from any race's results), ATTRACT\n(the demo), and the AI HUB SESSION (online ghost race, inside the hub only).\n\n**Content:** 4 tracks of 3.8–5.0 km, 6 teams with distinct craft, 12 pilots, 4 speed classes, 10 weapons, 6 songs,\n10 season events plus a 4-race Grand Prix, an 11-drill academy, an ending.\n\n**Controls.** The controller (DualSense, standard mapping) comes first. The keyboard is native. The mouse drives the\nmenus and fires.\n\n| Action | Controller | Keyboard / mouse |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Steer | Left stick ←→ (analog), d-pad ←→ | A / D (also ← / →) |\n| Pitch nose down / up | Left stick ↑ / ↓, d-pad ↑ / ↓ | ↑ / ↓ |\n| Thrust (hold) | ✕ | W |\n| Ai","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a73e9f9c-f8ef-4903-be97-67cc0d869215","uuid":"d15c0f9d-1619-4f44-a61a-6ca2321ec636","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:46:56.383Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"nal CDN dependencies. Do not read files outside what you need - other agents share this directory. They share this machine’s ports and processes too. Prefer file:// and the engine’s headless tools. If you need a server (the AI Hub harness does), bind 127.0.0.1 on a port the OS picks (`python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` prints it) and confirm the page is your ga