{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018MrDn782aTgrFHmGU2ZYuu","type":"tool_result","content":"1:# SNIPEOUT HD\n28:## 0. How to use this plan\n48:## 1. The game on one page\n103:## 2. Tech stack and architecture\n105:### 2.1 Stack (pinned)\n144:### 2.2 Dev folder layout\n201:### 2.3 The page and the bundle\n250:### 2.4 Units, axes, conventions (bugs hide here: unit-test them first)\n273:### 2.5 Modules and their public surfaces\n311:### 2.6 Scenes (engine scenes) and the flow\n335:### 2.7 Engine changes (make them in M0–M2; mark each `// SNIPEOUT EDIT:`)\n367:### 2.8 Determinism rules\n379:## 3. Systems\n381:### 3.1 Track system (`20-track.js`)\n467:### 3.2 Craft physics and handling (`25-ship.js`, `27-collide.js`)\n607:### 3.3 Race director and modes (`40-race.js`)\n651:### 3.4 Weapons and pickups (`30-weapons.js`, `06-data-weapons.js`)\n687:### 3.5 AI pilots (`35-ai.js`)\n738:### 3.6 Cameras and the TV director (`60-render.js`)\n781:### 3.7 Rendering (`60-render.js`, `62-shipkit.js`, `64-trackmesh.js`, `66-scenery.js`, `68-fx.js`)\n897:### 3.8 HUD (`70-hud.js`): pixel art at game resolution, anchored to `r.W × r.H`\n928:### 3.9 Front-end, UI and UX: 10-foot and controller-first (`75-ui.js`, `55-glyphs.js`, `01-input.js`)\n1009:### 3.10 Audio (`50-audio.js`, `10-data-music.js`): all synthesized, no files\n1094:### 3.11 Narrative and writing style (`09-data-story.js`)\n1126:### 3.12 Season, progression, saves (`08-data-season.js`, `78-season-ui.js`)\n1147:### 3.13 Replays, ghosts, records (`45-replay.js`)\n1160:### 3.14 Attract mode (`85-attract.js`)\n1181:### 3.15 Dev mode and live tuning (`80-dev.js`, `SN.Tune` in `00-core.js`)\n1198:### 3.16 Multiplayer: local split screen and AI Hub sessions\n1254:### 3.17 Settings: the game's own, beside the engine's standard ones\n1275:### 3.18 About / Credits and the exit interview (`11-data-about.js`)\n1317:## 4. Content\n1319:### 4.1 Teams, craft, pilots, principals (`05-data-teams.js`)\n1380:### 4.2 Speed classes\n1398:### 4.3 Tracks (`07-data-tracks.js`): layouts checked during planning\n1404:#### PORT SOLENNE: tier 1, beginner. 3.84 km · tightest 56 m (lighthouse hairpin) · slopes ±5 % (−15 % only inside the jump) · 1 drop jump\n1448:#### KARAKUM SPIRE: tier 1, intermediate. 4.59 km · tightest 55 m · a gorge **gap** jump · a spiral that passes over its own track (24.5 m clearance)\n1496:#### MERIDIAN STACK: tier 2, advanced. 4.18 km · tightest 52 m · the Stack **drop** · a figure-eight crossover (31.9 m clearance) · night\n1540:#### BOREALIS VAULT: tier 3, expert. 5.03 km · tightest 44 m (switchbacks) · climbs to +11 % · the Vault **drop** (17.2 m) · a 490 m ice tunnel\n1584:### 4.4 Weapon icons and pickup look\n1605:### 4.5 Season events, story beats, academy drills, radio (`08-data-season.js`, `09-data-story.js`)\n1661:### 4.6 Sponsors and signage (`66-scenery.js` canvas textures)\n1685:## 5. Build order and milestones\n1716:## 6. Risks and how to de-risk them\n1736:## 7. Testing (one command)\n1746:### 7.1 Harness\n1766:### 7.2 Node suites (fast, about 60 s in all)\n1809:### 7.3 The player bot (`tests/lib/bot.mjs`)\n1816:### 7.4 Scripted playthrough of the whole game (the season suite and the `playthrough` test)\n1834:### 7.5 Browser suites (Playwright, Chromium headless; `tests/browser/`)\n1853:### 7.6 AI Hub tests\n1880:### 7.7 Screenshots (only for what scripts can't judge)\n1901:## 8. Definition of done: every TASK.md requirement, where it lives, how it is proven\n1931:## Appendix A: reference code (use as is)\n1993:## Appendix B: gibberish voice (`SN.Voice`)\n2019:## Appendix C: planning-script results (`tools/trackcheck.mjs` must reproduce them)\n2034:## Appendix D: AI Hub session skeleton (`90-hub.js`)\n2058:## Appendix E: World3D facts learned in planning (saves the builders an afternoon)\n23:Working Environment and deliverable:\nWorking Environment and deliverable:\nCreate a subfolder called [GameTitle-SOURCE-claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-20261103-184651] to keep all your dev files in. You can develop separate modules, then bundle into a single file [GameTitle-SOURCE-claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-20261103-184651].html in root when you’re done. You may use external CDN dependencies. Do not read files outside what you need - other agents share this directory. They share this machine’s ports and processes too. Prefer file:// and the engine’s headless tools. If you need a server (the AI Hub harness does), bind 127.0.0.1 on a port the OS picks (`python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` prints it) and confirm the page is your game before trusting a result. Stop only processes you started, by their PID: never pkill, killall, fuser -k, or kill whatever holds a port.\n\nPerformance: Treat the performance budget as unlimited. Add as much detail, enemies, effects, lights, and shadows as the scene needs. Out of scope (handled later): FPS targets, optimization.\n\nTesting: Test as much as you like with headless tools: scripts that play the game and check its state (the engine’s game.advance and game.debug, as in game/test.mjs), including a scripted playthrough of the whole game. Keep them behind one command that prints only failures and a one-line count, and rerun it only after changing the code it covers. Use screenshots only for what a script can’t check. Don’t use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a73e9f9c-f8ef-4903-be97-67cc0d869215","uuid":"95b9632f-1ed0-4abb-a8de-368316146c31","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:46:58.689Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"1:# SNIPEOUT HD\n28:## 0. How to use this plan\n48:## 1. The game on one page\n103:## 2. Tech stack and architecture\n105:### 2.1 Stack (pinned)\n144:### 2.2 Dev folder layout\n201:### 2.3 The page and the bundle\n250:### 2.4 Units, axes, conventions (bugs hide here: unit-test them first)\n273:### 2.5 Modules and their public surfaces\n311:### 2.6 Scenes (engine scenes) and the flow\n335