{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015rU3cLfv95oP6p6n2SkP7c","type":"tool_result","content":"/*!\nmy-3D2dge 3D v0.85.2: real 3D worlds under my-3D2dge games (three.js r182 and Rapier 0.19.3)\nhttps://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge (MIT License)\n\nA 3D world, its physics and its camera under a my-3D2dge game. The 2D engine (engine/my-3d2dge.js) still runs the game:\nits exact fixed steps, scenes, input, pause menu, settings, touch buttons, sound, the rigs and their moves, the HUD. This\nmodule simulates and draws the world under the game's clear canvas. Everything is drawn by code: levels written as text,\ntextures pixel by pixel, characters by the 2D engine's rigs (as cards, or as 3D puppets on the same joints), props and\npickups from four shapes. No image, model or sound files.\n\nAGENTS: this header is the whole manual for the 3D side; the API card (API.md) covers the 2D side (E.Game, input, rigs,\nmoves, E.font, E.ui, E.Menu, audio). Read the code below to debug it, or to change it (it is yours to change, in any\nway you see fit): its sections start with \"/* ===\" and a number.\n\n## Host it (served: browsers load modules only over http; npx serve at the repository's root, or my-3d2dge.vercel.app)\n<script type=\"importmap\">{ \"imports\": { \"three\": \"/vendor/three-0.182.0/three.webgpu.min.js\",\n  \"three/webgpu\": \"/vendor/three-0.182.0/three.webgpu.min.js\", \"three/tsl\": \"/vendor/three-0.182.0/three.tsl.min.js\",\n  \"@dimforge/rapier3d-simd-compat\": \"/vendor/rapier3d-simd-compat-0.19.3/rapier.js\" } }</script>\n<style>html, body { margin: 0; height: 100%; overflow: hidden; background: #000 }</style>\n<div id=\"stage\" style=\"position:relative;width:100%;height:100%;overflow:hidden\">\n  <canvas id=\"view\"></canvas><canvas id=\"screen\" style=\"position:absolute;inset:0;width:100%;height:100%\"></canvas></div>\n<script src=\"/dist/my-3d2dge.js\"></script>                   the 2D engine, a classic script, first\n<script type=\"module\" src=\"/dist/my-3d2dge-3d.js\"></script>  this file\n<script type=\"module\" src=\"game.js\"></script>                your game, a module (const E = My3D2dge;)\n\n## Mental model\n- The 2D engine's units: x east, y south, z up; 16 units = 1 metre = 1 tile; speeds in units a second; angles in\n  radians from +x toward +y (0 east, PI/2 south, -PI/2 north).\n- new E.Game({ ..., bg: null }): its canvas is clear, and the HUD draws on it over the 3D picture (r.overlay, r.text,\n  E.ui, E.font) in game pixels, r.W x r.H. res: 'ps1', expand: true as in the 2D games.\n- await E.World3D.init(game) once (a boot step), then new E.World3D(game, { level, ... }) for each level. In a scene:\n  update(dt) { your rules, then world.step(dt) } and draw(r) { world.draw(r), then your HUD }. Paused or frozen\n  (game.freeze), update doesn't run, so the world holds still and still draws. A frame between steps draws the actors\n  and the camera part way from the step before (game.alpha: even motion at 144 Hz); a pose set in onDraw is placed as set.\n- Deterministic: the world steps in the game's fixed steps; gameplay randomness comes from E.rng(seed); the camera's\n  pose is worked out in the steps. world.reset() then the same inputs give the same world.hash() on WebGPU, WebGL 2 and\n  run again. Gameplay never reads the picture or the GPU: it reads actors, props and the level.\n- The camera decides what \"up the stick\" means: move with world.camera.ground(game.input.move()).\n\n## Quick start: a complete game (collect the gems, mind the slimes)\n```js quickstart\nconst E = My3D2dge;\nconst game = new E.Game({ canvas: 'screen', res: 'ps1', expand: true, bg: null, input: E.World3D.INPUT, menu: { settings: E.World3D.SETTINGS } });\nconst LEVEL = { rows: [\n  '################',\n  '#t............t#',\n  '#..g.....==..g.#',\n  '#........==....#',\n  '#..s.....^^....#',\n  '#.............s#',\n  '#..g...@....g..#',\n  '#t............t#',\n  '################'],\n  legend: { '=': { floor: 16 }, '^': { stairs: 'n', floor: 16 }, g: { mark: 'gem' }, s: { mark: 'slime' }, '@': { mark: 'start' } } };\nlet world, hero, slimes, gems, hp, done;\nconst move = [0, 0];\nconst play = {\n  view: 'iso', views: ['iso', 'threequarter', 'topdown'], pausable: true, touch: ['jump'], camera: { zoom: [.6, 2] },\n  enter() {\n    world.reset(); const rnd = E.rng(1), s = world.marks.start[0];\n    hero = world.hero({ x: s.x, y: s.y, rig: new E.Humanoid({ colors: { cloth: '#2f8f86' } }) });\n    slimes = world.marks.slime.map(m => world.mob({ x: m.x, y: m.y, blob: new E.Blob({ R: 6 }) }));\n    gems = world.marks.gem.map(m => world.thing('gem', m.x, m.y, m.z + 2, { spin: .5, bob: 3 }));\n    world.camera.follow(hero); hp = 3; done = false;\n    for (const e of slimes) e.data.t = rnd() * 2;\n  },\n  update(dt) {\n    if (game.input.pressed('camera')) world.camera.set(world.camera.mode === 'view' ? 'chase' : 'view');\n    world.camera.ground(game.input.move(), move);\n    hero.vx = E.approach(hero.vx, move[0] * 90, 900 * dt); hero.vy = E.approach(hero.vy, move[1] * 90, 900 * dt);\n    if (Math.hypot(move[0], move[1]) > .1) hero.facing = Math.atan2(move[1], move[0]);\n    if (hero.grounded && game.input.pressed('jump')) hero.jump(170);\n    for (const e of slimes) {   // hop at the hero now and then; touch one: a heart lost, knocked back\n      if ((e.data.t -= dt) < 0 && e.grounded) { e.data.t = 1.2; const a = Math.atan2(hero.y - e.y, hero.x - e.x); e.vx = Math.cos(a) * 60; e.vy = Math.sin(a) * 60; e.vz = 140; e.facing = a; }\n      if (Math.hypot(e.x - hero.x, e.y - hero.y) < 11 && Math.abs(e.z - hero.z) < 10 && !(hero.data.inv > 0)) { hp--; hero.data.inv = 1; hero.push((hero.x - e.x) * 12, (hero.y - e.y) * 12, 120); game.shake(3); }\n    }\n    hero.data.inv = (hero.data.inv || 0) - dt;\n    for (const t of world.touching(hero, 'gem')) { world.remove(t); gems.splice(gems.indexOf(t), 1); game.particles.glints(t.x, t.y, t.z + 6, 8, '#bff8ff'); }\n    if (!done && (!gems.length || hp <= 0)) { done = true; game.note(gems.length ? 'OUCH!' : 'ALL GEMS!', 2); game.after(2, () => game.go('play')); }\n    world.step(dt);\n  },\n  draw(r) {\n    world.draw(r);\n    r.overlay(g => { E.ui.hearts(g, 6, 6, Math.max(0, hp), 3); E.font.text(g, 'GEMS ' + gems.length, 6, 18, '#bff8ff'); });\n  }\n};\ngame.start({ scenes: { play }, scene: 'play', boot: { steps: [\n  ['The 3D engine', () => E.World3D.init(game)],\n  ['The level', () => { world = new E.World3D(game, { level: LEVEL }); world.prepare(); }]] } });\n```\n\n## The level: text, one character per tile; row 0 is north\nlevel: { rows, legend, ground, side, colors, bottom }. A legend entry says what its character is (the defaults: '#' wall\n48, 'w' wall 12, 'P' pillar 56, '.' floor, ' ' pit, 't' brazier):\n  { floor: h }        walkable at height h (0 the ground; above 0 a raised floor, solid to the ground)\n  { wall: h }         a solid block h tall, cut low where it stands between a view camera and the level (cut: false)\n  { pillar: h }       each block of these tiles is one pillar, cut to a stump when it hides the target\n  { stairs: 'n'|'s'|'e'|'w', from: 0, floor: 16 }   rising toward that side, from height `from` to `floor`\n  { platform: h, thick: 4 }   a slab in the air, its top at h (walk under it); add pit: true for nothing below\n  { pit: true }       no floor: a body that falls in is lost (actor.fell)\n  { torch: true }     a brazier with a flickering light (the lights go to the torches nearest the target)\n  { mark: 'name' }    a spot: world.marks.name -> [{ x, y, z, cx, cy }] (the tile's centre, on its surface)\n  { shape: 'bench', facing, scale, mats, solid }   a shape standing on the tile (world.shape), walked round when solid\nand its look: ground ('flagstone' 'grass' 'dirt' 'sand' 'snow' 'planks' 'tiles' 'plain' 'water' 'lava', or (x, y) =>\n[r, g, b]) with colors { base, dark, light }, side ('brick' 'block' 'earth' 'planks' 'metal') with color (and top, line).\nThe level's ground is every tile's default; its side styles the floors' edges, its wallSide the walls ('brick'; a tile's\nown side wins). Add looks: E.World3D.GROUND.name, E.World3D.SIDE.name.\n\n## The world: new E.World3D(game, o)\no: { level, look: 'card' | 'puppet', res: 'pixels' | 'balanced' | 'full', gravity: 480, sky: '#06050b' or [top,\nhorizon], fog: { color, near, far } (units past the target) or false, ambient: [sky, ground, intensity], sun: { color,\nintensity, dir: [x, y, z] (it shines along it), area, shadow }, torchLights: 6, torchShadows: 1, torchPower: 30,\nlights: 2 (your own), spots: 0 (flashlights), glowLights: 4, shadows, outlines, filter, camera: { mode, proj, fov, chase, ... }, terrain (an open\nworld instead of the level: below), overlay: { fov } (first person: below), onDraw: (world, r) => {} (your own 3D drawing\neach frame, whichever scene draws the world) }\n  world.step(dt) / world.draw(r) / world.reset() / world.hash([your numbers]) / world.prepare() (build the shaders now)\n  world.dispose(): frees a world (its physics, meshes and textures); dispose a level's world before making the next\n  world.width, depth (units), mw, mh (tiles), bottom, marks, torches, actors, props, platforms, things, time, stats\n  world.ground(x, y)   the ground's height (a wall's top on a wall; bottom over a pit)\n  world.floorAt(x, y, z)   the surface a body at height z stands on or falls to (platforms too)\n  world.topH(x, y)     the top of whatever stands there (shots stop below it)\n  world.tile(cx, cy), spec(cx, cy), solidTile(cx, cy)   the level's character, its legend entry, walls and pillars\n  world.flow() -> flow: flow.to(x, y) aims it; flow.dir(x, y, out) -> the way there ([x, y], or null: go straight);\n      it climbs stairs and gentle slopes and drops off ledges, never jumps\n  world.randomFloor(rnd, minDist, fx, fy) -> [x, y]; world.clear(x0, y0, z0, x1, y1, z1) -> a clear line of sight?\n  world.ray(x, y, z, dx, dy, dz, len, { skip: actor(s), level: true (sight lines), down }) -> the first hit { x, y, z, nx,\n      ny, nz, dist, actor, prop, ground } or null: hitscan, ledges, feet on the ground\n  world.block({ x, y, z, w, d, h, yaw } | { x, y, z, r, h }): an invisible fixed collider (walls you draw your own way;\n      z its base, as for world.box, world.thing and world.shape)\n  world.near(x, y, r, kind) -> actors within r; world.touching(actor, kind) -> things it touches\n  world.project(x, y, z) -> [x, y] in game pixels for the HUD (null behind the camera)\n  world.remove(actor | prop | platform | thing)\n\n## Actors: world.hero(o) and world.mob(o)\nhero: a kinematic capsule walked by Rapier's character controller: it slides along walls, climbs stairs and slopes,\nsteps up curbs, rides moving platforms, pushes crates and shoves mobs. mob: a dynamic body; the crowd pushes and piles\nup by itself, knockback is momentum. o: { x, y, z, rig: new E.Humanoid(...) | blob: new E.Blob(...) | orb: { color, r },\nr, half (the capsule: radius, half its straight part; half 0 a ball), facing, fly (no gravity), mass, trail (a color for\nthe rig's weapon trail), look ('card' | 'puppet' for this one), shadow, pose: false (you pose it yourself) }\nEach step you set what it wants: a.vx, a.vy (units a second on the ground: E.approach toward a target speed), a.jump(v)\nor a.vz, a.facing, a.anim (what its rig does: { attack: move.state, pose: 'cheer' | 'die' | 'cast', hurt, dash, expr };\na blob's { squash, look: [dx, dy], squint, flap }); world.step moves it, reads back a.x, a.y, a.z (its feet), a.vx,\na.vy, a.vz, a.grounded, a.landed (this step), a.fell (dropped below the level), and poses its rig.\n  a.place(x, y, z), a.push(vx, vy, vz) (knockback), a.setSolid(false) (fallen: it stops blocking the others), a.remove(),\n  a.resize(half) (a crouch, a slide: the capsule shorter, the feet kept; look up with world.ray before standing)\n  a.flash (a hit flash while above 0: count it down yourself, a.flash -= dt), a.fade (0..1), a.sink (units drawn into the floor: spawning, sinking away), a.visible,\n  a.data (yours). E.Attack, E.Combo and E.MOVES work as in 2D: attack.hits(list, e => E.inArc(a, a.facing, e, range,\n  half), e => ...) on actors.\n\n## Props, platforms and things\n  world.shape(id, x, y, z, { facing, scale, mats, flip, params, solid: true }): a shape (docs/ASSETS.md: an E.SHAPES id,\n  a store set's 'SET:id' after E.addAssets, or an entry) in 3D, from the 2D engine's parts and materials, solid as\n  map.solid's box. A reset takes yours away (the legend's stand again); world.shapes holds them; world.remove(s)\n  world.crate(x, y, z, yaw), world.barrel(x, y, z): dynamic bodies (z their centre; left out, on the floor); world.knock(p, fromX, fromY, dv, up) throws one;\n  world.box({ x, y, z, w, d, h, color, side, fixed, mass }); a prop has x, y, z, q (its rotation), data\n  world.platform({ w, d, h, path: [[x, y, z], ...] (its top's points), speed, wait, loop, side, color }): a platform that\n  moves (there and back, or round with loop); heroes on it ride it, bodies on it are carried\n  world.thing(kind, x, y, z, o): drawn by code: coin gem heart key star potion chest door flag sign lever pad spring\n  tree bush rock mushroom fence crystal trunk (an item box) typewriter (a save point); o: { yaw, spin (turns a second),\n  bob, size, color, open (0..1), on, solid: true | [w, d, h] (a block where it stands: world.setSolid(t, false) opens\n  it), data }. Add a kind:\n  E.World3D.THINGS.lamp = (pen, t, time) => { pen.tube(0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 20, 1, '#555').glow(0, 0, 21, 2, '#ffd36a'); }\n  A door: { w (32, across its local x), h (40), swing: true (leaves on hinges; else a gate that rises), locked: 'iron' (a\n  key's id) }. world.reach(hero, 14, kinds) -> the thing in front of it within reach (what a check button acts on);\n  world.unlock(door, inv) -> 'open' | 'unlocked' (the key taken from an E.RPG inventory) | 'locked';\n  world.through(door, hero, { then }): the door opening between rooms (Resident Evil's): the camera goes through as it\n  swings open, the hero comes out beyond it; while world.passing the world holds still: skip your rules\n\n## Drawing for one frame (in draw, before world.draw(r))\n  world.arc(x, y, z, r0, r1, a0, a1, color, alpha): a flat arc on the ground (a telegraph)\n  world.glow(x, y, z, r, color, { core, light: true }): a glowing ball (a bolt); the first four asked for light the scene\n  world.pen.at(x, y, z, yaw, size, pitch, roll).box(x, y, z, w, d, h, color).ball(x, y, z, rx, ry, rz, color).tube(x0, y0, z0, x1,\n      y1, z1, r, color).cone(...base to tip).bar(x0, y0, z0, x1, y1, z1, w, t, color).glow(x, y, z, r, color)\n      .slab(x0, y0, z0, x1, y1, z1, ax, ay, az, w, t, color) (a box from one point to the other, w wide along a: any lean)\n  world.light({ color, reach, power, flicker }) (made once, with the world): a light; set its x, y, z and on each frame\n  world.flashlight(hero, { color, reach, angle, power, flicker: 0..1 (a failing bulb) }) (a world made with { spots: 1 }):\n  a spot from its hand along its facing, every frame, casting shadows; .on\n  game.particles (sparks, dust, bits, rings, glints, fire, text): drawn in 3D; they land on the level; text goes on the HUD\n  game.shake(n), game.flash(color), game.freeze(s), game.note(text): as in 2D\n\n## The camera: world.camera\n  modes: 'view' (the 2D engine's views as real 3D cameras following camera.target: game.view (game.nextView()),\n  game.yaw (game.rotateView(45)): the starter's shell binds V and [ ] to them, the engine no key; game.zoom (the wheel,\n  a pinch, Settings, where the scene's camera.zoom allows); proj 'persp' | 'ortho'; dist; walls in\n  front cut low), 'chase' (behind the target; it swings round behind as the target moves; a click captures the mouse to\n  turn it), 'first' (the target's eyes), 'fly', 'fixed' (camera.fixed = { x, y, z, yaw, pitch, roll, fov }: your own),\n  'zones' (Resident Evil's: camera.zones = [{ box: [x0, y0, x1, y1], at: [x, y, z], look: [x, y, z] (fixed) | follow:\n  true (turning after the target) | rail: [[x, y, z], [x, y, z]] (sliding level with it), fov (framed for 4:3: a taller\n  screen widens it) }]: the target's zone is the camera, cut once it is camera.margin (8) units in (camera.zone,\n  camera.cuts); across a cut ground() keeps the old way while the stick is held)\n  camera: { capture: true (a click captures the mouse in any mode; camera.takeMouse(out) -> its [dx, dy] since the last\n  call: turn your own camera), near, far (units) }; camera.target also sizes the outlines and centres the sun's shadow\n  camera.set(mode), camera.follow(actor), camera.ground(move, out) -> the stick turned to the ground,\n  camera.aim(actor) -> the angle the mouse (on the floor) or right stick points from it, or null; camera.heading,\n  camera.pose, camera.chase = { pitch, dist, fov, height, auto }, camera.first = { pitch, fov }\n  camera.steer(hero, move, dt, out) -> the way to go by the player's setting (game.settings.define(E.World3D.CONTROLS)):\n  ground(), or tank controls (up forward along hero.facing, down back, left and right turn it: set facing yourself only\n  when !camera.tank)\n  Lock-on (Zelda's targeting): camera.lock(hero, mobs, { range: 160 }) -> the one in sight (alive, in range, a clear\n  line) nearest where hero faces, or null; camera.cycle(mobs, 1 | -1) the next round the hero; camera.unlock();\n  camera.lockOn. The chase and first cameras swing behind the hero to frame both, the view frames both, ground() strafes\n  round it, a reticle marks it (camera.reticle: a color, false); it lets go when the target dies (alive = false), leaves\n  or goes past 1.25 times the range. Turn the hero to it yourself\n\n## Open worlds: new E.World3D(game, { terrain: { height: (x, y) => z, ... } })\nterrain: { size (32768: two kilometres), height, color: (x, y, z, nz, out) => [r, g, b] (0..255), cell (32), chunk (32\ncells), far (draw distance), physics (heightfields this near an actor), detail (1.7), rock: { colors, band, slope: [s0,\ns1] } (strata where it steepens), speckle, mottle, bottom }. Streamed: a chunk is sampled when first asked about, its\ncollider made near actors (world.step), its meshes near the camera (world.draw: finer near). world.ground, floorAt, topH\nand world.terrain.normal(x, y) read the triangles Rapier collides with; flow() goes straight. sky: { top, horizon, ground,\nsun, haze } is a dome; fog: { color, near, far }. Keep height() fast: it runs per sample.\n\n## Vehicles, tracks, flight (the RACING and FLIGHT slices show them)\nworld.vehicle({ kind: 'kart' | 'car' | 'hover', x, y, z, angle, rig (its driver, seated), color, and the drive module's\nE.Car options over the kind's (top, accel, brake, turn, grip, drift, hop...), surfaces, ground: (x, y) => tag }): a ball\nRapier rolls (walls, bumps, ramps, jumps) steered by E.Car's arcade handling; an actor. Each step v.update(dt, null,\n{ steer, gas, brake, drift }) (or an input), then world.step; it reads speed, slide, angle, drifting, charge (a kart's\ndrift boost), boostT, onGround, surface ('road', 'boost', a track's off, the level's ground: grass, sand... slow it),\nbumped; v.boost(sec, power), v.spinOut(sec). E.Race (the drive module) races vehicles as it races E.Cars.\nworld.track({ path: new E.Path(points [x, y, z], { closed: true, smooth: 'catmull' }), width: 96, bank: .25 (radians at\nthe tightest bends), walls: true, kerb: 8, pads: [[d, across]] (boost pads), gaps: [[d0, d1, side]] (no wall: a\nshortcut's way off), off (the surface beside it, 'grass'; null: the ground's own), surface: 'road' (a shortcut's\n'dirt'), color, line }): the road and its walls, drawn\nand solid (heights in the path: hills, a hump to jump; banked about the low edge); track.at(d) -> { x, y, z, angle, bank\n}, track.where(x, y) -> { d, across, z }, track.surface(x, y). A terrain under it keeps below it (your height function).\nworld.flyer({ x, y, z, yaw, speed: 300, min, max (brake, boost), turn, climb, bank, invert, low (least height), bounds:\n[x0, y0, z0, x1, y1, z1] | rail: { path, speed, w, h } (Star Fox: the rail's point rides on, the ship moves about a w x h\nwindow across it) }): each step f.update(dt, { steer: [x, y], boost, brake, roll: -1 | 1 (a barrel roll: f.rolling),\nloop }); f.x, y, z, yaw, pitch, roll, speed, frame (the rail's point), hitGround; f.seek(list, { cone, range }): what a\ncharged shot locks on; f.at(): where to draw it this frame. world.pen.at(x, y, z, yaw, size, pitch, roll) tips the pen.\ncamera 'behind': behind the target's heading (yaw, angle or facing) and pitch, swinging after it (camera.behind = { dist,\nheight, ahead, lag, fov, roll }): a kart's camera, a ship's (follow f.frame on a rail).\nSplit screen: game.splitScreen(2) and world.addCamera(o): viewport i draws through world.cameras[i]; world.project\nanswers in the viewport drawn last; no filter while split.\n\n## First person and third: a shooter (the starter's SHOOTER slice shows it all)\ncamera 'fixed' with capture: each step turn by camera.takeMouse(), set camera.fixed (the eyes, or over the shoulder pulled\nin by world.ray). overlay: { fov } makes world.overlay = { scene, camera, pen, on }: drawn after the world, its depth\ncleared (the gun never sinks into a wall), lit like the world. overlay.pen is a pen in the eye's frame (engine units: x\nahead, y right, z up): draw the arms and gun in onDraw; world.pen there draws bodies (the pens empty after each frame).\n\n## The look\n  world.look = 'card' | 'puppet'; world.res = 'pixels' | 'balanced' | 'full'; world.shadows; world.outlines\n  world.filter = { look: 'clean' | 'cel' (Comic cel: flat bands of light, ink) | 'pixel' (big dithered pixels), apply:\n  'all' | 'objects' | 'env', bloom, fxaa, bands, ink, inkW, punch, px, levels, dither }\n  E.World3D.SETTINGS in the game's menu lets the player pick the look, the picture, shadows and the filter.\n  E.World3D.INPUT: WASD or arrows move, Space jumps, J or a click attacks, Shift dashes, E skill, Q lock, C camera.\n\n## Your own three.js\nE.THREE (three/webgpu), E.TSL, E.RAPIER; world.scene, world.pw (Rapier's world), E.World3D.toThree(x, y, z) (engine\nunits to three's metres, y up). The rules: WebGPURenderer and node materials only (MeshLambertNodeMaterial,\nMeshToonNodeMaterial, MeshBasicNodeMaterial, TSL): never WebGLRenderer, ShaderMaterial, onBeforeCompile, EffectComposer or\nthree/addons (not vendored); no compute shaders or storage buffers; nothing read back from the GPU. An InstancedMesh with\nover 1,000 instances never uses DynamicDrawUsage (r182 drops its updates). Make meshes and lights when the world is made,\nnot mid-game (a new material builds its shader: a stall).\n\n## Tests\nHeadless Chromium runs it on WebGL 2 (?backend=webgl) and WebGPU (SwiftShader). world.hash() is the proof; game.advance(s)\nruns fixed steps at once; game.input.hold / tap script input. The kits expose window.__game.\n*/","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a73e9f9c-f8ef-4903-be97-67cc0d869215","uuid":"40cee48b-75fb-41c6-a0d5-c26321c2b036","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:47:11.529Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"/*!\nmy-3D2dge 3D v0.85.2: real 3D worlds under my-3D2dge games (three.js r182 and Rapier 0.19.3)\nhttps://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge (MIT License)\n\nA 3D world, its physics and its camera under a my-3D2dge game. The 2D engine (engine/my-3d2dge.js) still runs the game:\nits exact fixed steps, scenes, input, pause menu, settings, touch buttons, sound, the rigs and their moves, the HUD. This\nmodule simulates and draws the world under the game's clear canvas. Everything is drawn by code: levels written as text,\ntextures pixel by pixel, characters by the 2D engine's rigs (as cards, or as 3D puppets on the same joints), props and\npickups from four shapes. No image, model or sound files.\n\nAGENTS: this header is the whole manual for the 3D side; the API card (API.md) covers the 2D side (E.Game, input, rigs,\nmoves, E.font, E.ui, E.Menu, audio). Read the code below to debug it, or to change it (it is yours to change, in any\nway you see fit): its sections start with \"/* ===\" and a number.\n\n## Host it (served: browsers load modules only over http; npx serve at the repository's root, or my-3d2dge.vercel.app)\n<script type=\"importmap\">{ \"imports\": { \"three\": \"/vendor/three-0.182.0/three.webgpu.min.js\",\n  \"three/webgpu\": \"/vendor/three-0.182.0/three.webgpu.min.js\", \"three/tsl\": \"/vendor/three-0.182.0/three.tsl.min.js\",\n  \"@dimforge/rapier3d-simd-compat\": \"/vendor/rapier3d-simd-compat-0.19.3/rapier.js\" } }</script>\n<style>html, body { margin: 0; height: 100%; overflow: hidden; background: #000 }</style>\n<div id=\"stage\" style=\"position:relative;width:100%;height:100%;overflow:hidden\">\n  <canvas id=\"view\"></canvas><canvas id=\"screen\" style=\"position:absolute;inset:0;width:100%;height:100%\"></canvas></div>\n<script src=\"/dist/my-3d2dge.js\"></script>                   the 2D engine, a classic script, first\n<script type=\"module\" src=\"/dist/my-3d2dge-3d.js\"></script>  this file\n<script type=\"module\" src=\"game.js\"></script>                your game, a module (const E = My3D2dge;)\n\n## Mental model\n- The 2D engine's units: x east, y south, z up; 16 units = 1 metre = 1 tile; speeds in units a second; angles in\n  radians from +x toward +y (0 east, PI/2 south, -PI/2 north).\n- new E.Game({ ..., bg: null }): its canvas is clear, and the HUD draws on it over the 3D picture (r.overlay, r.text,\n  E.ui, E.font) in game pixels, r.W x r.H. res: 'ps1', expand: true as in the 2D games.\n- await E.World3D.init(game) once (a boot step), then new E.World3D(game, { level, ... }) for each level. In a scene:\n  update(dt) { your rules, then world.step(dt) } and draw(r) { world.draw(r), then your HUD }. Paused or frozen\n  (game.freeze), update doesn't run, so the world holds still and still draws. A frame between steps draws the actors\n  and the camera part way from the step before (game.alpha: even motion at 144 Hz); a pose set in onDraw is placed as set.\n- Deterministic: the world steps in the game's fixed steps; gameplay randomness comes from E.rng(seed); the camera's\n  pose is worked out in the steps. world.reset() then the same inputs give the same world.hash() on WebGPU, WebGL 2 and\n  run again. Gameplay never reads the picture or the GPU: it reads actors, props and the level.\n- The camera decides what \"up the stick\" means: move with world.camera.ground(game.input.move()).\n\n## Quick start: a complete game (collect the gems, mind the slimes)\n```js quickstart\nconst E = My3D2dge;\nconst game = new E.Game({ canvas: 'screen', res: 'ps1', expand: true, bg: null, input: E.World3D.INPUT, menu: { settings: E.World3D.SETTINGS } });\nconst LEVEL = { rows: [\n  '################',\n  '#t............t#',\n  '#..g.....==..g.#',\n  '#........==....#',\n  '#..s.....^^....#',\n  '#.............s#',\n  '#..g...@....g..#',\n  '#t............t#',\n  '################'],\n  legend: { '=': { floor: 16 }, '^': { stairs: 'n', floor: 16 }, g: { mark: 'gem' }, s: { mark: 'slime' }, '@': { mark: 'start' } } };\nlet world, hero, slimes, gems, hp, done;\nconst move = [0, 0];\nconst play = {\n  view: 'iso', views: ['iso', 'threequarter', 'topdown'], pausable: true, touch: ['jump'], camera: { zoom: [.6, 2] },\n  enter() {\n    world.reset(); const rnd = E.rng(1), s = world.marks.start[0];\n    hero = world.hero({ x: s.x, y: s.y, rig: new E.Humanoid({ colors: { cloth: '#2f8f86' } }) });\n    slimes = world.marks.slime.map(m => world.mob({ x: m.x, y: m.y, blob: new E.Blob({ R: 6 }) }));\n    gems = world.marks.gem.map(m => world.thing('gem', m.x, m.y, m.z + 2, { spin: .5, bob: 3 }));\n    world.camera.follow(hero); hp = 3; done = false;\n    for (const e of slimes) e.data.t = rnd() * 2;\n  },\n  update(dt) {\n    if (game.input.pressed('camera')) world.camera.set(world.camera.mode === 'view' ? 'chase' : 'view');\n    world.camera.ground(game.input.move(), move);\n    hero.vx = E.approach(hero.vx, move[0] * 90, 900 * dt); hero.vy = E.approach(hero.vy, move[1] * 90, 900 * dt);\n    if (Math.hypot(move[0], move[1]) > .1) hero.facing = Math.atan2(move[1], move[0]);\n    if (hero.grounded && game.input.pressed('jump')) hero.jump(170);\n    for (const e of slimes) {   // hop at the hero now and then; touch one: a heart lost, knocked back\n      if ((e.data.t -= dt) < 0 && e.grounded) { e.data.t = 1.2; const a = Math.atan2(hero.y - e.y, hero.x - e.x); e.vx = Math.cos(a) * 60; e.vy = Math.sin(a) * 60; e.vz = 140; e.facing = a; }\n      if (Math.hypot(e.x - hero.x, e.y - hero.y) < 11 && Math.abs(e.z - hero.z) < 10 && !(hero.data.inv > 0)) { hp--; hero.data.inv = 1; hero.push((hero.x - e.x) * 12, (hero.y - e.y) * 12, 120); game.shake(3); }\n    }\n    hero.data.inv = (hero.data.inv || 0) - dt;\n    for (const t of world.touching(hero, 'gem')) { world.remove(t); gems.splice(gems.indexOf(t), 1); game.particles.glints(t.x, t.y, t.z + 6, 8, '#bff8ff'); }\n    if (!done && (!gems.length || hp <= 0)) { done = true; game.note(gems.length ? 'OUCH!' : 'ALL GEMS!', 2); game.after(2, () => game.go('play')); }\n    world.step(dt);\n  },\n  draw(r) {\n    world.draw(r);\n    r.overlay(g => { E.ui.hearts(g, 6, 6, Math.max(0, hp), 3); E.font.text(g, 'GEMS ' + gems.length, 6, 18, '#bff8ff'); });\n  }\n};\ngame.start({ scenes: { play }, scene: 'play', boot: { steps: [\n  ['The 3D engine', () => E.World3D.init(game)],\n  ['The level', () => { world = new E.World3D(game, { level: LEVEL }); world.prepare(); }]] } });\n```\n\n## The level: text, one character per tile; row 0 is north\nlevel: { rows, legend, ground, side, colors, bottom }. A legend entry says what its character is (the defaults: '#' wall\n48, 'w' wall 12, 'P' pillar 56, '.' floor, ' ' pit, 't' brazier):\n  { floor: h }        walkable at height h (0 the ground; above 0 a raised floor, solid to the ground)\n  { wall: h }         a solid block h tall, cut low where it stands between a view camera and the level (cut: false)\n  { pillar: h }       each block of these tiles is one pillar, cut to a stump when it hides the target\n  { stairs: 'n'|'s'|'e'|'w', from: 0, floor: 16 }   rising toward that side, from height `from` to `floor`\n  { platform: h, thick: 4 }   a slab in the air, its top at h (walk under it); add pit: true for nothing below\n  { pit: true }       no floor: a body that falls in is lost (actor.fell)\n  { torch: true }     a brazier with a flickering light (the lights go to the torches nearest the target)\n  { mark: 'name' }    a spot: world.marks.name -> [{ x, y, z, cx, cy }] (the tile's centre, on its surface)\n  { shape: 'bench', facing, scale, mats, solid }   a shape standing on the tile (world.shape), walked round when solid\nand its look: ground ('flagstone' 'grass' 'dirt' 'sand' 'snow' 'planks' 'tiles' 'plain' 'water' 'lava', or (x, y) =>\n[r, g, b]) with colors { base, dark, light }, side ('brick' 'block' 'earth' 'planks' 'metal') with color (and top, line).\nThe level's ground is every tile's default; its side styles the floors' edges, its wallSide the walls ('brick'; a tile's\nown side wins). Add looks: E.World3D.GROUND.name, E.World3D.SIDE.name.\n\n## The world: new E.World3D(game, o)\no: { level, look: 'card' | 'puppet', res: 'pixels' | 'balanced' | 'full', gravity: 480, sky: '#06050b' or [top,\nhorizon], fog: { color, near, far } (units past the target) or false, ambient: [sky, ground, intensity], sun: { color,\nintensity, dir: [x, y, z] (it shines along it), area, shadow }, torchLights: 6, torchShadows: 1, torchPower: 30,\nlights: 2 (your own), spots: 0 (flashlights), glowLights: 4, shadows, outlines, filter, camera: { mode, proj, fov, chase, ... }, terrain (an open\nworld instead of the level: below), overlay: { fov } (first person: below), onDraw: (world, r) => {} (your own 3D drawing\neach frame, whichever scene draws the world) }\n  world.step(dt) / world.draw(r) / world.reset() / world.hash([your numbers]) / world.prepare() (build the shaders now)\n  world.dispose(): frees a world (its physics, meshes and textures); dispose a level's world before making the next\n  world.width, depth (units), mw, mh (tiles), bottom, marks, torches, actors, props, platforms, things, time, stats\n  world.ground(x, y)   the ground's height (a wall's top on a wall; bottom over a pit)\n  world.floorAt(x, y, z)   the surface a body at height z stands on or falls to (platforms too)\n  world.topH(x, y)     the top of whatever stands there (shots stop below it)\n  world.tile(cx, cy), spec(cx, cy), solidTile(cx, cy)   the level's character, its legend entry, walls and pillars\n  world.flow() -> flow: flow.to(x, y) aims it; flow.dir(x, y, out) -> the way there ([x, y], or null: go straight);\n      it climbs stairs and gentle slopes and drops off ledges, never jumps\n  world.randomFloor(rnd, minDist, fx, fy) -> [x, y]; world.clear(x0, y0, z0, x1, y1, z1) -> a clear line of sight?\n  world.ray(x, y, z, dx, dy, dz, len, { skip: actor(s), level: true (sight lines), down }) -> the first hit { x, y, z, nx,\n      ny, nz, dist, actor, prop, ground } or null: hitscan, ledges, feet on the ground\n  world.block({ x, y, z, w, d, h, yaw } | { x, y, z, r, h }): an invisible fixed collider (walls you draw your own way;\n      z its base, as for world.box, world.thing and world.shape)\n  world.near(x, y, r, kind) -> actors within r; world.touching(actor, kind) -> things it touches\n  world.project(x, y, z) -> [x, y] in game pixels for the HUD (null behind the camera)\n  world.remove(actor | prop | platform | thing)\n\n## Actors: world.hero(o) and world.mob(o)\nhero: a kinematic capsule walked by Rapier's character controller: it slides along walls, climbs stairs and slopes,\nsteps up curbs, rides moving platforms, pushes crates and shoves mobs. mob: a dynamic body; the crowd pushes and piles\nup by itself, knockback is momentum. o: { x, y, z, rig: new E.Humanoid(...) | blob: new E.Blob(...) | orb: { color, r },\nr, half (the capsule: radius, half its straight part; half 0 a ball), facing, fly (no gravity), mass, trail (a color for\nthe rig's weapon trail), look ('card' | 'puppet' for this one), shadow, pose: false (you pose it yourself) }\nEach step you set what it wants: a.vx, a.vy (units a second on the ground: E.approach toward a target speed), a.jump(v)\nor a.vz, a.facing, a.anim (what its rig does: { attack: move.state, pose: 'cheer' | 'die' | 'cast', hurt, dash, expr };\na blob's { squash, look: [dx, dy], squint, flap }); world.step moves it, reads back a.x, a.y, a.z (its feet), a.vx,\na.vy, a.vz, a.grounded, a.landed (this step), a.fell (dropped below the level), and poses its rig.\n  a.place(x, y, z), a.push(vx, vy, vz) (knockback), a.setSolid(false) (fallen: it stops blocking the others), a.remove(),\n  a.resize(half) (a crouch, a slide: the capsule shorter, the feet kept; look up with world.ray before standing)\n  a.flash (a hit flash while above 0: count it down yourself, a.flash -= dt), a.fade (0..1), a.sink (units drawn into the floor: spawning, sinking away), a.visible,\n  a.data (yours). E.Attack, E.Combo and E.MOVES work as in 2D: attack.hits(list, e => E.inArc(a, a.facing, e, range,\n  half), e => ...) on actors.\n\n## Props, platforms and things\n  world.shape(id, x, y, z, { facing, scale, mats, flip, params, solid: true }): a shape (docs/ASSETS.md: an E.SHAPES id,\n  a store set's 'SET:id' after E.addAssets, or an entry) in 3D, from the 2D engine's parts and materials, solid as\n  map.solid's box. A reset takes yours away (the legend's stand again); world.shapes holds them; world.remove(s)\n  world.crate(x, y, z, yaw), world.barrel(x, y, z): dynamic bodies (z their centre; left out, on the floor); world.knock(p, fromX, fromY, dv, up) throws one;\n  world.box({ x, y, z, w, d, h, color, side, fixed, mass }); a prop has x, y, z, q (its rotation), data\n  world.platform({ w, d, h, path: [[x, y, z], ...] (its top's points), speed, wait, loop, side, color }): a platform that\n  moves (there and back, or round with loop); heroes on it ride it, bodies on it are carried\n  world.thing(kind, x, y, z, o): drawn by code: coin gem heart key star potion chest door flag sign lever pad spring\n  tree bush rock mushroom fence crystal trunk (an item box) typewriter (a save point); o: { yaw, spin (turns a second),\n  bob, size, color, open (0..1), on, solid: true | [w, d, h] (a block where it stands: world.setSolid(t, false) opens\n  it), data }. Add a kind:\n  E.World3D.THINGS.lamp = (pen, t, time) => { pen.tube(0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 20, 1, '#555').glow(0, 0, 21, 2, '#ffd36a'); }\n  A door: { w (32, across its local x), h (40), swing: true (leaves on hinges; else a gate that rises), locked: 'iron' (a\n  key's id) }. world.reach(hero, 14, kinds) -> the thing in front of it within reach (what a check button acts on);\n  world.unlock(door, inv) -> 'open' | 'unlocked' (the key taken from an E.RPG inventory) | 'locked';\n  world.through(door, hero, { then }): the door opening between rooms (Resident Evil's): the camera goes through as it\n  swings open, the hero comes out beyond it; while world.passing the world holds still: skip your rules\n\n## Drawing for one frame (in draw, before world.draw(r))\n  world.arc(x, y, z, r0, r1, a0, a1, color, alpha): a flat arc on the ground (a telegraph)\n  world.glow(x, y, z, r, color, { core, light: true }): a glowing ball (a bolt); the first four asked for light the scene\n  world.pen.at(x, y, z, yaw, size, pitch, roll).box(x, y, z, w, d, h, color).ball(x, y, z, rx, ry, rz, color).tube(x0, y0, z0, x1,\n      y1, z1, r, color).cone(...base to tip).bar(x0, y0, z0, x1, y1, z1, w, t, color).glow(x, y, z, r, color)\n      .slab(x0, y0, z0, x1, y1, z1, ax, ay, az, w, t, color) (a box from one point to the other, w wide along a: any lean)\n  world.light({ color, reach, power, flicker }) (made once, with the world): a light; set its x, y, z and on each frame\n  world.flashlight(hero, { color, reach, angle, power, flicker: 0..1 (a failing bulb) }) (a world made with { spots: 1 }):\n  a spot from its hand along its facing, every frame, casting shadows; .on\n  game.particles (sparks, dust, bits, rings, glints, fire, text): drawn in 3D; they land on the level; text goes on the HUD\n  game.shake(n), game.flash(color), game.freeze(s), game.note(text): as in 2D\n\n## The camera: world.camera\n  modes: 'view' (the 2D engine's views as real 3D cameras following camera.target: game.view (game.nextView()),\n  game.yaw (game.rotateView(45)): the starter's shell binds V and [ ] to them, the engine no key; game.zoom (the wheel,\n  a pinch, Settings, where the scene's camera.zoom allows); proj 'persp' | 'ortho'; dist; walls in\n  front cut low), 'chase' (behind the target; it swings round behind as the target moves; a click captures the mouse to\n  turn it), 'first' (the target's eyes), 'fly', 'fixed' (camera.fixed = { x, y, z, yaw, pitch, roll, fov }: your own),\n  'zones' (Resident Evil's: camera.zones = [{ box: [x0, y0, x1, y1], at: [x, y, z], look: [x, y, z] (fixed) | follow:\n  true (turning after the target) | rail: [[x, y, z], [x, y, z]] (sliding level with it), fov (framed for 4:3: a taller\n  screen widens it) }]: the target's zone is the camera, cut once it is camera.margin (8) units in (camera.zone,\n  camera.cuts); across a cut ground() keeps the old way while the stick is held)\n  camera: { capture: true (a click captures the mouse in any mode; camera.takeMouse(out) -> its [dx, dy] since the last\n  call: turn your own camera), near, far (units) }; camera.target also sizes the outlines and centres the sun's shadow\n  camera.set(mode), camera.follow(actor), camera.ground(move, out) -> the stick turned to the ground,\n  camera.aim(actor) -> the angle the mouse (on the floor) or right stick points from it, or null; camera.heading,\n  camera.pose, camera.chase = { pitch, dist, fov, height, auto }, camera.first = { pitch, fov }\n  camera.steer(hero, move, dt, out) -> the way to go by the player's setting (game.settings.define(E.World3D.CONTROLS)):\n  ground(), or tank controls (up forward along hero.facing, down back, left and right turn it: set facing yourself only\n  when !camera.tank)\n  Lock-on (Zelda's targeting): camera.lock(hero, mobs, { range: 160 }) -> the one in sight (alive, in range, a clear\n  line) nearest where hero faces, or null; camera.cycle(mobs, 1 | -1) the next round the hero; camera.unlock();\n  camera.lockOn. The chase and first cameras swing behind the hero to frame both, the view frames both, ground() strafes\n  round it, a reticle marks it (camera.reticle: a color, false); it lets go when the target dies (alive = false), leaves\n  or goes past 1.25 times the range. Turn the hero to it yourself\n\n## Open worlds: new E.World3D(game, { terrain: { height: (x, y) => z, ... } })\nterrain: { size (32768: two kilometres), height, color: (x, y, z, nz, out) => [r, g, b] (0..255), cell (32), chunk (32\ncells), far (draw distance), physics (heightfields this near an actor), detail (1.7), rock: { colors, band, slope: [s0,\ns1] } (strata where it steepens), speckle, mottle, bottom }. Streamed: a chunk is sampled when first asked about, its\ncollider made near actors (world.step), its meshes near the camera (world.draw: finer near). world.ground, floorAt, topH\nand world.terrain.normal(x, y) read the triangles Rapier collides with; flow() goes straight. sky: { top, horizon, ground,\nsun, haze } is a dome; fog: { color, near, far }. Keep height() fast: it runs per sample.\n\n## Vehicles, tracks, flight (the RACING and FLIGHT slices show them)\nworld.vehicle({ kind: 'kart' | 'car' | 'hover', x, y, z, angle, rig (its driver, seated), color, and the drive module's\nE.Car options over the kind's (top, accel, brake, turn, grip, drift, hop...), surfaces, ground: (x, y) => tag }): a ball\nRapier rolls (walls, bumps, ramps, jumps) steered by E.Car's arcade handling; an actor. Each step v.update(dt, null,\n{ steer, gas, brake, drift }) (or an input), then world.step; it reads speed, slide, angle, drifting, charge (a kart's\ndrift boost), boostT, onGround, surface ('road', 'boost', a track's off, the level's ground: grass, sand... slow it),\nbumped; v.boost(sec, power), v.spinOut(sec). E.Race (the drive module) races vehicles as it races E.Cars.\nworld.track({ path: new E.Path(points [x, y, z], { closed: true, smooth: 'catmull' }), width: 96, bank: .25 (radians at\nthe tightest bends), walls: true, kerb: 8, pads: [[d, across]] (boost pads), gaps: [[d0, d1, side]] (no wall: a\nshortcut's way off), off (the surface beside it, 'grass'; null: the ground's own), surface: 'road' (a shortcut's\n'dirt'), color, line }): the road and its walls, drawn\nand solid (heights in the path: hills, a hump to jump; banked about the low edge); track.at(d) -> { x, y, z, angle, bank\n}, track.where(x, y) -> { d, across, z }, track.surface(x, y). A terrain under it keeps below it (your height function).\nworld.flyer({ x, y, z, yaw, speed: 300, min, max (brake, boost), turn, climb, bank, invert, low (least height), bounds:\n[x0, y0, z0, x1, y1, z1] | rail: { path, speed, w, h } (Star Fox: the rail's point rides on, the ship moves about a w x h\nwindow across it) }): each step f.update(dt, { steer: [x, y], boost, brake, roll: -1 | 1 (a barrel roll: f.rolling),\nloop }); f.x, y, z, yaw, pitch, roll, speed, frame (the rail's point), hitGround; f.seek(list, { cone, range }): what a\ncharged shot locks on; f.at(): where to draw it this frame. world.pen.at(x, y, z, yaw, size, pitch, roll) tips the pen.\ncamera 'behind': behind the target's heading (yaw, angle or facing) and pitch, swinging after it (camera.behind = { dist,\nheight, ahead, lag, fov, roll }): a kart's camera, a ship's (follow f.frame on a rail).\nSplit screen: game.splitScreen(2) and world.addCamera(o): viewport i draws through world.cameras[i]; world.project\nanswers in the viewport drawn last; no filter while split.\n\n## First person and third: a shooter (the starter's SHOOTER slice shows it all)\ncamera 'fixed' with capture: each step turn by camera.takeMouse(), set camera.fixed (the eyes, or over the shoulder pulled\nin by world.ray). overlay: { fov } makes world.overlay = { scene, camera, pen, on }: drawn after the world, its depth\ncleared (the gun never sinks into a wall), lit like the world. overlay.pen is a pen in the eye's frame (engine units: x\nahead, y right, z up): draw the arms and gun in onDraw; world.pen there draws bodies (the pens empty after each frame).\n\n## The look\n  world.look = 'card' | 'puppet'; world.res = 'pixels' | 'balanced' | 'full'; world.shadows; world.outlines\n  world.filter = { look: 'clean' | 'cel' (Comic cel: flat bands of light, ink) | 'pixel' (big dithered pixels), apply:\n  'all' | 'objects' | 'env', bloom, fxaa, bands, ink, inkW, punch, px, levels, dither }\n  E.World3D.SETTINGS in the game's menu lets the player pick the look, the picture, shadows and the filter.\n  E.World3D.INPUT: WASD or arrows move, Space jumps, J or a click attacks, Shift dashes, E skill, Q lock, C camera.\n\n## Your own three.js\nE.THREE (three/webgpu), E.TSL, E.RAPIER; world.scene, world.pw (Rapier's world), E.World3D.toThree(x, y, z) (engine\nunits to three's metres, y up). The rules: WebGPURenderer and node materials only (MeshLambertNodeMaterial,\nMeshToonNodeMaterial, MeshBasicNodeMaterial, TSL): never WebGLRenderer, ShaderMaterial, onBeforeCompile, EffectComposer or\nthree/addons (not vendored); no compute shaders or storage buffers; nothing read back from the GPU. An InstancedMesh with\nover 1,000 instances never uses DynamicDrawUsage (r182 drops its updates). Make meshes and lights when the world is made,\nnot mid-game (a new material builds its shader: a stall).\n\n## Tests\nHeadless Chromium runs it on WebGL 2 (?backend=webgl) and WebGPU (SwiftShader). world.hash() is the proof; game.advance(s)\nruns fixed steps at once; game.input.hold / tap script input. The kits expose window.__game.\n*/","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_015rU3cLfv95oP6p6n2SkP7c","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgh3ZEKZNjgiaZ7661tRY","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Nabc3pHoDpCT2XdSFCpN6r","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-sonnet-5-5-medium.build__20261103-184651__xa320fb/work/SnipeoutHD-WipeoutHD-claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-20261103-184651; sed -n 1,200p engine/handoff/API.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1360,"cache_read_input_tokens":39124,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1360},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a73e9f9c-f8ef-4903-be97-67cc0d869215","uuid":"05931238-d54f-40b0-a436-a4b2c89c6161","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:47:12.107Z","request_id":"req_011Cgh3ZE2hnChpoodg8Rxaf","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01Nabc3pHoDpCT2XdSFCpN6r":{"command":"cd /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-sonnet-5-5-medium.build__20261103-184651__xa320fb/work/SnipeoutHD-WipeoutHD-claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-20261103-184651; sed -n 1,200p engine/handoff/API.md"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Nabc3pHoDpCT2XdSFCpN6r","type":"tool_result","content":"<persisted-output>\nOutput too large (41.2KB). Full output saved to: /home/micha/.claude/projects/-home-micha-dev-cc-matrix-runs-claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-build--20261103-184651--xa320fb-work/a73e9f9c-f8ef-4903-be97-67cc0d869215/tool-results/bxc9jmr45.txt\n\nPreview (first 2KB):\n# my-3D2dge API card (v0.85.2)\n\nmy-3D2dge (\"My 3D 2D Game Engine\") is a general-purpose retro-modern 2D game engine built for AI models. Use it to port, remaster, reimagine and remix games from the 8-bit to 64-bit eras (NES, SNES, Genesis, N64, PS1), or to make spiritual successors to them. Everything is drawn by code: no image or sound files. Characters are 3D skeletons drawn as pixel art, and the camera can be any classic 2D view.\n\n**Start from a kit:** each genre has one, a complete small game written to be copied (the Genre playbook below names it). In a single file it sits between `GAME START` and `GAME END`: the starter's shell (the game, its title menu, the proof), the slice and the start; a genre kit holds one slice, `dist/my-3d2dge.html` every one and the ANIMATION LAB (how each animation is driven). In the handoff package each kit's game is `kits/<id>.js`, readable. **To make a game, change the slice into yours, with its own title, play and game-over scenes, and delete the starter's menu and the slices you do not use. The engine is yours to change too:** fix, extend or rewrite any of it as you see fit.\n\n**Looking a name up:** grep `API-INDEX.md` (every public name, one line; members are filed under instance names: `rig.`, `talk.`, `mover.`, `flow.`, `map.`), not the engine's source. It, the guide (`AI_GUIDE.md`) and the tools are in the handoff package (my-3d2dge.vercel.app/handoff/) and the repository.\n\n## Quick start (a complete game)\n```js\n(() => {\nconst E = My3D2dge, { px, clamp } = E;            // always start like this\nconst game = new E.Game({ canvas: 'screen', res: 'snes', view: 'threequarter' });\nconst map = new E.TileMap({\n  rows: ['##########', '#@...s...#', '#..##....#', '#........#', '##########'],\n  legend: { '#': 1, '@': 'hero', 's': 'slime' },  // number = wall type, string = spawn tag\n  types: { 1: { h: 30, top: '#57506a', side: '#3d3750' } },\n...\n</persisted-output>","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a73e9f9c-f8ef-4903-be97-67cc0d869215","uuid":"a20068eb-79ad-41ac-9ba8-2a6b425b0b83","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:47:12.127Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"# my-3D2dge API card (v0.85.2)\n\nmy-3D2dge (\"My 3D 2D Game Engine\") is a general-purpose retro-modern 2D game engine built for AI models. Use it to port, remaster, reimagine and remix games from the 8-bit to 64-bit eras (NES, SNES, Genesis, N64, PS1), or to make spiritual successors to them. Everything is drawn by code: no image or sound files. Characters are 3D skeletons drawn as pixel art, and the camera can be any classic 2D view.\n\n**Start from a kit:** each genre has one, a complete small game written to be copied (the Genre playbook below names it). In a single file it sits between `GAME START` and `GAME END`: the starter's shell (the game, its title menu, the proof), the slice and the start; a genre kit holds one slice, `dist/my-3d2dge.html` every one and the ANIMATION LAB (how each animation is driven). In the handoff package each kit's game is `kits/<id>.js`, readable. **To make a game, change the slice into yours, with its own title, play and game-over scenes, and delete the starter's menu and the slices you do not use. The engine is yours to change too:** fix, extend or rewrite any of it as you see fit.\n\n**Looking a name up:** grep `API-INDEX.md` (every public name, one line; members are filed under instance names: `rig.`, `talk.`, `mover.`, `flow.`, `map.`), not the engine's source. It, the guide (`AI_GUIDE.md`) and the tools are in the handoff package (my-3d2dge.vercel.app/handoff/) and the repository.\n\n## Quick start (a complete game)\n```js\n(() => {\nconst E = My3D2dge, { px, clamp } = E;            // always start like this\nconst game = new E.Game({ canvas: 'screen', res: 'snes', view: 'threequarter' });\nconst map = new E.TileMap({\n  rows: ['##########', '#@...s...#', '#..##....#', '#........#', '##########'],\n  legend: { '#': 1, '@': 'hero', 's': 'slime' },  // number = wall type, string = spawn tag\n  types: { 1: { h: 30, top: '#57506a', side: '#3d3750' } },\n  floorTex: (x, y) => E.tex.flagstone(x, y, { stones: ['#4b4559', '#554f64'].map(E.hex), mortar: E.hex('#221e2b'), hi: E.hex('#6f6782'), lo: E.hex('#322d3e'), speck: E.hex('#2a2634') })\n});\ngame.cam.bounds = v => map.bounds(v);\nconst s = map.find('hero'), hero = new E.Body({ x: s.x, y: s.y, r: 5 });\nconst rig = new E.Humanoid({ hat: 'pointed' });\ngame.start({\n  update(dt) {\n    const m = game.input.move(), d = game.view.screenDirToGround(m[0], m[1]);\n    hero.vx = d[0] * 80; hero.vy = d[1] * 80; hero.update(dt, map);\n    if (Math.hypot(d[0], d[1]) > .1) hero.facing = Math.atan2(d[1], d[0]);\n    rig.update(dt, { x: hero.x, y: hero.y, z: hero.z, vx: hero.vx, vy: hero.vy, facing: hero.facing || 0 });\n    game.focus(hero.x, hero.y, 10);                // the camera looks here: call every update\n  },\n  draw(r) {\n    map.drawFloor(r); r.shadow(hero.x, hero.y, 5); map.queueWalls(r);\n    r.actor(hero.x, hero.y, hero.z, (g, ox, oy) => rig.draw(g, ox, oy, r.view));\n    r.text('HELLO', 4, 4, '#ffffff');              // HUD text in screen pixels\n  }\n});\n})();\n```\n\n## Look target (read this before drawing anything)\nAim above SNES, level with the best PS1 / N64 2D games (Symphony of the Night, Metal Slug, Legend of Mana, Klonoa). Games that skip these steps look like Atari games. **Every game should use all of them:**\n1. **Resolution** `res: 'ps1'` (320x240) or `'snes'`, with `expand: true` so it fills any screen (anchor the HUD to `r.W` / `r.H`), and characters 40-60 px tall: `new E.Humanoid({ size: 1.3 })` (HD style is the default: shaded volumetric limbs, outfits, hair).\n2. **Dress the character**: an `outfit`, `hair`, a `hat`, `armor`, a `cape`, `sleeves` (Characters), and a real palette in `colors` (never leave the defaults on everyone).\n3. **Backdrop behind every side-view level**: `const bg = new E.Backdrop('castle-night')` then `bg.draw(r)` first in `draw` (a ground view's floor runs on to the screen's edges: `map.outside`). Presets: `day dusk night castle-night city-night desert forest ocean cave space`, or your own `{ sky, sun | moon, stars, starsY: .7, layers: [{ kind: 'mountains' | 'hills' | 'forest' | 'city' | 'castle' | 'clouds' | 'sea' | 'fog' | 'stalactites', color, y, height, parallax, fill }] }` (`starsY` = how far down the stars reach; a layer but clouds, fog and stalactites fills the screen below its skyline unless `fill: false`).\n4. **Textured tiles**: PlatformMap styles `stone` (with `moss`), `brick`, `wood`, `metal`, `ground` (with `flower`), `block`, `pipe`, `bonus`; TileMap floors from `E.tex.*`, TileMap walls with `face: 'brick' | 'stone' | 'rock' | 'plank' | 'timber' | 'plaster' | 'hedge'` and tops with `roof: 'tiles' | 'slab' | 'leaves' | 'grass'` (a village = timber and plaster houses with tile roofs, hedges, rock cliffs).\n5. **Props in every room** (3-8 per screen): `r.prop(name, x, y, z, { size, color, light: true })` with `torch candle candelabra chandelier brazier window banner pillar statue crate barrel chest pot sign fence lamp door tree pine bush flowers grass rock crystal bones skull vines cobweb lantern streetlamp awning hydrant trashcan firedrum sandbags cactus palm mushroom`. Flames animate, glow and can light the scene. `chest` / `door` take `{ open: true }`, `pillar` takes `{ broken: true }`, `window` is a gothic rose window with lead cames (`colors: [...]` for the glass, `size: 2-3` for a cathedral); hang `vines`, `cobweb`, `lantern` with `{ anchor: 'top' }`. With heroes at size 1.2+, give the scene `propSize: 1.4` (or set `E.style.propSize`) so props match them.\n6. **Effects on every hit**: `particles.explosion(x, y, z, size)`, `smoke`, `fire`, `glints`, `sparks`, `game.flash(color, s)`, `game.freeze`, `game.shake`.\n7. **Color**: `E.tones(hex)` gives `{ deep, sh, base, lt, hi }` hue-shifted shades and `E.ramp(hex, n)` a palette ramp. Shade everything you draw yourself with them (shadow side `sh`, lit edge `lt`, a `hi` sparkle).\n8. **Typography**: the default font is a 5x7 proportional font with lower case. Titles: `E.font.title(g, 'NAME', x, y, { scale: 3, colors: [...], align: 'center' })`. HUD text: `{ shadow: '#000', outline: false }` or `gradient: [top, bottom]`. `font: 'tiny'` is the old 3x5 font.\n9. **Light every scene**: `game.lights.enabled = true; game.lights.ambient = .5` in a scene's `enter`, then each draw a few lights where light comes from (`r.prop('lamp', x, y, 0, { light: true })`, `game.lights.add(x, y, z, 60, .9, { color })`): dusk, torchlight and glows give depth. It looks the same in every browser and check; `game.enableGPU({ map })` adds shadows in ground views, and falls back to it.\n10. **Faces in dialog**: `talk.say(lines, { name: 'MIRA', portrait: miraRig })` puts a live close-up of that Humanoid (blinking, hair, hat) beside every line. `rig.drawPortrait(g, x, y, 40)` draws one anywhere (HUD, RPG party windows).\n\n## Coordinates: what x, y, z mean in each view\nWorld units: one tile = 16. **z is always up.** Game logic never stores screen pixels.\n\n| View | Looks like | Ground plane | Use for levels | Move with |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `'iso'` (yaw 45, pitch 30) | Diablo, Landstalker | x/y ground, z up | `E.TileMap` | `E.Body` |\n| `'threequarter'` (pitch 55) | Zelda LttP, Stardew | x right, y down the screen | `E.TileMap` | `E.Body` |\n| `'topdown'` (pitch 80) | GTA 1, Hotline Miami | x right, y down | `E.TileMap` | `E.Body` |\n| `'brawler'` (pitch 25) | Final Fight, 2.5D | x right, y into the screen | `E.TileMap` or `E.PlatformMap` | `E.Body` or `E.Platformer` |\n| `'side'` (pitch 0) | Mario, Mega Man | **x right, z up, y = 0 (depth)** | `E.PlatformMap` | `E.Platformer` |\n| `'overhead'` (pitch 90, scale 1) | 1942, Galaga, Pac-Man | x = screen x px, y = screen y px | none, or a TileMap | your own x/y, or `E.Body` |\n\n- In `'side'` the screen shows x (right) and z (up). **y is depth and is normally 0.** Focus the camera with `game.focus(hero.x, 0, hero.z + 24)`.\n- In ground views, the screen direction the player pushes becomes a world direction through `game.view.screenDirToGround(mx, my)`.\n- `r.actor(x, y, z, (g, ox, oy) => ...)` calls your function with `(ox, oy)` = the buffer pixel where world point (x, y, z) lands. Draw the character around that point.\n\n## Rules\n1. Keep game logic in world units, and use `dt` everywhere. `update(dt)` runs in exact fixed steps: every 1/120 s of real time is one step of `dt = 1/120 * game.timeScale`, the same at any refresh rate (`game.alpha` = the part of a step left over).\n2. Draw only with `E.px.*`, `E.font`, `E.ui`, sprites, props and the `r.*` helpers. Never use `ctx.arc`, `ctx.stroke`, `ctx.fillText` or canvas gradients on the game buffer (they blur pixels). For translucency and glow use `px.blend(g, alpha, 'add' | 'multiply' | 'screen' | 'normal', () => {...})`, `px.polyDither`, `px.ddisc`, `r.glowDisc` and `r.shadow`: they use stepped alpha and blend modes like PS1 / SNES hardware (or dithering with `E.style.trans = 'dither'` for an NES / Genesis look).\n3. Anything that can overlap anything else goes through `r.actor`, `r.sprite` or `r.queue` (depth sorted). Backgrounds (`r.sky`, `map.drawFloor`, `level.draw`) come first in `draw`.\n4. Read input only in `update`. Use `pressed(a)` for one-shot actions (menus, jumps, shots), `down(a)` for held actions, and `buffered(a)` + `consume(a)` for forgiving combat input.\n5. Animate characters from continuous values (velocity, timers, easing): the rigs, not frame lists.\n6. Colors are `'#rrggbb'` (or `'#rgb'`) strings.\n7. Put the whole game in one IIFE `(() => { ... })();` between the markers, and start it with `game.start(...)`.\n\n## Common mistakes (check these first)\n- Nothing on screen: the camera looks at the wrong place. Use `game.follow(hero)` once (in the scene's `enter`), or call `game.focus(...)` every update. In side view the hero has y = 0.\n- Walls or level missing: you forgot `map.drawFloor(r)` + `map.queueWalls(r)` (TileMap) or `level.draw(r)` (PlatformMap).\n- Using a TileMap for a side-scroller. Side-scrollers use `E.PlatformMap` + `E.Platformer`.\n- HUD drawn in world coordinates. Use `r.text(...)` or `r.overlay(g => ...)` with screen pixels (`r.W`, `r.H`).\n- An old key layout: platformers want `input: 'PLATFORMER'` (Space jumps), shooters `input: 'SHMUP'` (Space fires).\n- Errors show in a red box on screen and in the console. Fix the first one first.\n\n## Game\n`new E.Game({ canvas: 'screen', view, views, res, expand, input, bg })`\n- `res`: `'nes'` 256x240, `'snes'` 256x224, `'genesis'` 320x224, `'gb'` 160x144, `'gba'` 240x160, `'ps1'` / `'n64'` 320x240, `'wide'` 400x225, or `[w, h]`. Fixed pixels: whole-number scaling on a desktop screen, the largest fit on a phone (Settings, Pixel scaling). Leave it out for an adaptive size.\n- `expand: true`: full screen at any shape, no bars. `res` becomes the least the screen shows and `r.W` x `r.H` grows to fill the window (a 16:9 monitor shows about 427x240 of `'ps1'`, a phone held upright about 320x690). Anchor the HUD to `r.W` and `r.H`, and give the level something to show in the extra space (more of it, a backdrop, a HUD deck under it on a tall screen). `expand: { maxW, maxH }` caps the growth. A scene may set it too.\n- Adaptive size: `minW` / `minH` set the scale (the screen shows at least that many pixels), `maxW` / `maxH` cap the view (the rest is letterboxed). `portrait: { maxH: 1000 }` overrides them while the screen is taller than wide, so a phone held upright (9:16) is filled instead of showing a band across its middle. `game.screen.portrait` says which way it is.\n- `input`: `'DEFAULT'` (top-down action), `'PLATFORMER'`, `'SHMUP'`, `'THIRDPERSON'`, or your own `{ action: ['KeyQ', 'Pad0'] }`.\n- `views`: the views your game supports; `game.nextView()` cycles them (bind it to a key).\n- Start: `game.start({ update(dt), draw(r) })`, or with scenes (below).\n- Methods: `follow(obj, { z: 16, lead: 20 })` (camera tracks it automatically), `focus(x, y, z)` (or aim it yourself every update), `setView(id)`, `nextView()`, `freeze(s)` (hit-stop: `update` waits; `game.hitstop` is the time left), `shake(n)`, `after(s, fn)`, `every(s, fn)` (timers in game time, return `{ cancel() }`), `mouseGround()`, `go(scene, data)`, `enableGPU({ map })`.\n- Fields: `W`, `H` (screen pixels), `time`, `real`, `timeScale` (0.25 = slow motion), `paused`, `input`, `audio`, `particles`, `lights`, `view`, `errors`, `cam`. `game.fail('my power', err)` reports an error in your code (logged, in `errors`, on the error panel) and plays on.\n- Camera zoom and turn: `game.setZoom(1.5)` (.5 .. 3; everything drawn by the engine re-rasterizes crisply, the HUD stays), `game.rotateView(45)` (ground views only), `game.resetCamera()` (to the scene's zoom), `game.zoom`, `game.yaw`, `game.note('ZOOM 2x')` (a short notice at the top of the screen; `game.shownNote` is the one showing). Custom painters can read `r.view.zoom` (undefined at 1x) to scale their own art.\n- The player's zoom: a scene's `camera: { zoom: [.75, 2], start: 1, tall: 1.5 }` lets the player zoom within `zoom` from `start` (wheel, `-` `=`, pinch, Settings; `false`: none; `AI_GUIDE.md`, Camera).\n- Tests and tools: `new E.Game({ headless: true })` (the default in Node: no screen, sound or DOM; `draw` is skipped) or `{ loop: false }` runs only by `game.advance(seconds, { draw })` (exact fixed steps at once); `input.tap(action)` presses for one step, `input.hold(action, on)`; `game.debug = { snapshot() {...} }` is your game's handle (`tools/check.mjs` prints it); `game.stop()`, `start()` (resumes), `dispose()`.\n- Sessions: `?record=1`, then the pause menu's Save session; `node tools/replay.mjs session.json` replays it exactly, headless (`AI_GUIDE.md`, Sessions).\n- Camera: `cam.offset = [dx, dy]` sits the focus that many screen pixels from the centre (above a phone's touch buttons: `[0, -60]`); `cam.bounds = v => map.bounds(v)` keeps it inside the level (in side views a level shorter than the screen sits on the bottom edge; `cam.align = 'center'` to change); `cam.room = [160, 128]` moves screen by screen (Zelda, Metroid) and `cam.moving` is true while it slides; `cam.smooth` = lag in seconds (0 = locked to the target, no lag); `cam.window = [48, 24]` a dead zone the focus moves in before the camera follows; `cam.snapY = true` follows a followed body's height only when it lands (side-scrollers); `cam.frame([p1, p2], { margin, zoom: [.5, 1] })` keeps them all in view, zooming out as they part (`frame(null)` stops); `cam.free = { keys, edge: 10, drag, speed: 300, bounds: [x0, y0, x1, y1] }` a free camera (strategy, sims): keys or a pad's stick, the screen's edge, a drag pan it (a finger pans by its drag, never as the touch stick), a zoom goes toward the pointer or the pinch, in every view and turn.\n\n## Scenes (title, play, game over)\n```js\ngame.start({ scene: 'title', scenes: {\n  title: { update(dt) { if (game.input.pressed('start')) game.go('play', { level: 1 }); },\n           draw(r) { r.sky(['#101848', '#402a70']); r.text('PRESS START', r.W / 2, 100, '#fff', { align: 'center', scale: 2 }); } },\n  play: { pausable: true, view: 'side', input: 'PLATFORMER', res: 'snes', views: ['side', 'brawler'], touch: ['jump', 'attack'],\n          enter(data) { /* build the level */ }, exit() {}, update(dt) {}, draw(r) {} }\n} });\n```\n`game.go(name, data)` switches before the next step, through a short dithered fade (`game.fadeTime`; `{ fade: 0 }` cuts; `{ fade: 'wipe' | 'iris' | 'mosaic' | 'swirl' | 'dissolve' | 'white', seconds: .4, at: hero }` a transition, a plain fade under reduce motion); timers and particles are cleared. `pausable: true` lets Escape / P / Start pause it and opens the pause menu (below). `view`, `views`, `input`, `res`, `expand`, `touch`, `propSize` are applied on entry. A scene's `back()` takes a phone's back gesture first (return true when it went back somewhere: a sub-menu closed).\n\n## Motion and timing: tweens, scripts, paths, weather\n- Tweens (Phaser's): `game.tween(door, { y: 40 }, .5, { ease: 'outBack', delay, repeat, yoyo, onDone, keep })` eases numbers on game time: paused with the game, slowed by `timeScale`, ended by a scene change unless `keep`. Returns `{ done, cancel() }`. `E.ease` has Penner's set: `linear`, `inSine` `outSine` `inOutSine`, `inQuad` `outQuad` `inOutQuad`, the same for `Cubic`, `Back`, `Elastic`, `Bounce`.\n- Scripts (Unity's coroutines) for cutscenes, boss patterns, wave timelines, tutorials:\n```js full-script\ngame.script(function* () {\n  yield 1;                                   // seconds of game time\n  yield E.until(() => door.open);            // a condition\n  yield game.tween(boss, { y: 80 }, 1.5);    // a tween (or another script, or a generator) finishing\n  for (let w = 0; w < 3; w++) { spawnWave(w); yield E.any([8, () => enemies.length === 0]); }\n});\n```\n  `E.all([...])` waits for all; a bare `yield` waits one step. It runs at once to its first `yield`, the same at any refresh rate; `{ keep: true }` outlives the scene; `.cancel()`, `.done`, `.value`.\n- Paths: `const p = new E.Path([[x, y, z], ...], { closed, smooth: 'catmull' })`: `p.length`, `p.at(d)` (`{ x, y, z, angle }` by distance along: moving platforms, dives, rails), `p.nearest(x, y)` (a racer's progress), `p.draw(r)` to see it.\n- Weather: `game.weather = { kind: 'rain' | 'snow' | 'ash' | 'leaves' | 'sand', amount: .8, wind: .3, lightning: .2 }` (null: clear) falls through the world in every view, settles or splashes, flashes and thunders; cosmetic only (its own random stream), and it ends with the scene: set it in `enter`.\n- Wrap-around (Asteroids): `x = E.wrap(x, 0, W)`, `E.wrapDiff(a, b, W)` (the short way across the seam), and `wrap: [W, H]` on `r.sprite` or `r.actor` draws a copy across each edge.\n\n## Standard features (every game has them: don't build your own)\nWith no code: a **pause menu** on pausable scenes (Resume, Settings, Controls, Full screen, Quit to title; keys, mouse, gamepad and touch), **settings** saved per game (`game.settings`: volume, music, effects, mute, reduce motion, screen shake and flashes, pixel scaling, a screen filter: clean, scanlines or CRT, the frame rate: Auto, 60 or Full, and its readout), **controls** the player rebinds (conflicts said first; dead zone, invert aim, button symbols, vibration; touch button side, size, opacity), **full screen** (a phone's first tap and each game started by a tap, a top-right button, the menu; turning it off is remembered), a **menu button** at the top right (on a touch screen it stays in play: it is the touch START), a **way out** of every menu screen (close, back, a sideways swipe), the **back gesture** stepping back in the game (`game.back()`: a menu screen closes, play pauses) before it leaves the page, **pausing** when the page is hidden or the controller unplugs, and a page set up for phones (safe areas, no scrolling, zooming or pull to refresh on the canvas).\n- Adjust: `new E.Game({ menu: { items: [['Restart', fn]], settings: [{ key: 'hints', label: 'Hints', value: true }, { key: 'difficulty', options: ['Easy', 'Normal', 'Hard'], value: 'Normal' }], controls: { attack: 'Sword', skill: false }, quit: 'title' } })`, then read `game.settings.hints`. Leave parts out: `menu: false` (your own pause), `fullscreen: 'button' | false`, `corner: false`, `backGesture: false`, `autoPause: false`. `game.cornerArea()`: where the corner's buttons cover the screen (or null): keep a HUD clear of it.\n- Save slots (`game.saves`): three slots and an autosave, each with its place, level and time played. `game.saves.write = () => ({ data, place, level })` and `read = save => {...}`; then `save(slot)` (0, 1, 2 or `'auto'`), `load(slot)`, `copy(a, b)`, `erase(slot)`, `autosave()`, `list()`, `latest()` (a title's Continue). `menu: { saves: true }` (or `{ slots, version, migrate(data, from) }`) puts Save (while `saves.canSave`: a save point) and Load in the pause menu, and `saves.show('save' | 'load')` opens the screen from your own menu. Blocked storage keeps them for the visit.\n- Prompts: `game.input.label('jump')` is that action's key or button on the player's device, after rebinding ('Space', 'A', '✕'). A canvas title's OPTIONS: `game.menu('settings')`. From code: `game.settings.set(key, v)`, `game.fullscreen.toggle()` (from a key, click or tap), `game.pause(reason)`, `game.resume()`.\n- **Boot**: heavy setup in steps behind a boot screen, and a report for any error before play: `game.start({ scenes, scene, boot: { title, steps: [['Building the city', () => { map = build(); }]] } })` (`AI_GUIDE.md`, Boot).\n\n## Input: `game.input`\n- Actions in every preset: `up down left right` (WASD, arrows, d-pad), `start` (Enter, Start), `pause` (Escape, P, Start), `confirm` (Enter, Space, J, Z, pad A), `cancel` (Escape, Backspace, X, K, pad B).\n- DEFAULT adds `attack` (J, X, click), `dash` (Space, Shift, K), `skill` (L, E, right click), `jump` (Z). PLATFORMER: `jump` (Space, Z, K, pad A), `attack` (X, J, click), `dash` (Shift, C, L), `skill`. SHMUP: `fire` (Space, Z, J, click), `bomb` (X, K). THIRDPERSON: `sprint` (Shift), `jump` (Space), `crouch` (C, Ctrl), `interact` (E, F), `attack` (J), `block` (K), `dodge` (L), `use` (Q), `map` (M, Tab).\n- `down(a)`, `pressed(a)`, `released(a)`, `repeat(a, delay, rate)` (menus), `buffered(a, win)` + `consume(a)`, `consumeAll()`, `anyPressed()`, `move()` (screen direction `[x, y]`, length up to 1), `use(preset)`, `touchButtons(['jump', 'attack'])`, `touchSet('confirm', 'TALK', near)` (a context button: relabel it, or hide it while it has nothing to do).\n- Moves: `input.motion('236', 'attack', { facing })` is true on the step a quarter circle forward and punch ends (numpad notation, mirrored when `facing` is -1: `'623'`, `'41236'`); `input.sequence(['up', 'up', 'down', 'down+right', 'attack'], { window: .5 })` any run of inputs (a cheat code); `input.history` the last second of them (`{ t, dir, pressed }`, for an input display).\n- Players (two to four on one machine): `game.players` is `[game.input, ...]`, each an input with this API, its `player` number, `name` (`'P2'`), `color` (`E.PLAYER_COLORS`) and device. `game.allowJoin({ max: 2, onJoin: (input, n) => {} })` lets them in: Start on a free gamepad, or 2 on the keyboard (player 2 takes its right half: arrows, `.` `/` `,` and the numpad; player 1 keeps WASD with F G H); `game.addPlayer({ pad: 0 })` or `({ keys: 'right' })`, `game.removePlayer(p)`. Prompts, the pause (P2 PAUSED), a lost controller and the Controls screen are per player. One screen: `cam.frame(heroes)`. Split: `game.splitScreen(2, { follow: [h1, h2], layout: 'auto' })` (`'columns'`, `'rows'`, `'grid'`; up to 4) draws the scene once per viewport (`r.viewport` is `{ i, x, y, w, h }`), then `scene.hud(r)` once over the whole screen; `game.viewports[i].follow(t)` or `.focus(x, y)`.\n- `clear()` releases held inputs, pending presses, virtual sticks and touch buttons; focus loss does this automatically. `suppress(a)` blocks an action for the current tick; `suppressCode('Mouse0')` lets a HUD widget consume that mouse button while other devices bound to the action still work.\n- Controller settings: `deadzone` (default `.18`, radial and rescaled), `invertAimY`, `lastDevice` (the index has the rest). The left stick and the touch stick, pushed past halfway, press `up` / `down` / `left` / `right` as the d-pad does (menus, dialog choices, ladders); bind `PadAxisUp/Down/Left/Right` to your own menu actions for the same.\n- Touch settings: `touchEnabled`, `stickSide` (`'left'` or `'right'`), `stickRadius` (CSS pixels), `touchFilter(event)` (return false to reserve a canvas tap for UI). `touchArea()`: where the touch buttons cover the screen (`{ x0, y0, x1, y1 }` in screen pixels, or null): keep a prompt clear of it, as a bottom `E.Dialog` does by itself. `clicked()`: where a click or tap on the canvas landed (`[x, y]` in screen pixels) for the step after it, else null, for HUD buttons of your own; `pointer()`: the mouse in screen pixels; `drag()`: how far the stick's finger moved this step (`[dx, dy]`, or null), to steer one to one; `steer = 'drag'`: a finger anywhere steers, no stick drawn (`stickOn`: a finger is on it). `zones = [{ x0, y0, x1, y1, act }]` (fractions of the canvas): a finger in one holds its act until it lifts, no stick (a pinball's halves, a rhythm game's lanes). Gestures (a mouse or one finger, screen pixels): `box()` → `{ x0, y0, x1, y1, done }` (a left drag; on touch a long press, then a drag), `longPress()`, `doubleTap()`, `pan()` → `[dx, dy]` (a finger's drag on open ground, a middle or right drag), `zoomPoint()`; `feed('down' | 'move' | 'up', x, y, { touch, button, id })` plays a pointer in a test. `bindButtons(root)` wires `[data-act]` elements with independent pointer tracking and cancellation cleanup.\n\n## Drawing: `r` in `draw(r)`\n- Background: `r.sky(['#top', '#mid', '#bottom'])`, `r.starfield({ count, speed, dir: 'down' | 'left' })`.\n- Depth sorted: `r.actor(x, y, z, (g, ox, oy) => rig.draw(g, ox, oy, r.view), { flash: '#ffffff', flashMix: .75, alpha, xray, outline, ghost, emissive, every, key })` (the flash tints over the sprite so shading shows: `flashMix` .5 by default, 1 solid; `every: 2, key: npc` redraws a background character every other frame; actors up to about 250 px tall), `r.sprite(x, y, z, sprite, { flip, anchor: 'bottom' | 'center', glow, flash })`, `r.queue(x, y, z, g => {...}, { bias })`, `r.actorNow(...)` (as `r.actor`, drawn at once over what is drawn so far), `r.box(g, x0, y0, z0, x1, y1, z1, top, side)` (inside a queue function). `{ pick: unit }` on any of them (and `r.shape`), then `r.pick(sx, sy)` → what the last frame drew under a screen point, front to back.\n- Floor marks: `r.shadow(x, y, radius, alpha, color, surfaceZ)`, `r.decal(g => r.groundArc(...))`, `r.groundDisc`, `r.groundRing`, `r.groundPts(x, y, rad, n, z)`.\n- On top, unlit, screen pixels: `r.text(str, x, y, color, { align, scale, outline })`, `r.textAt(x, y, z, str, color)`, `r.overlay(g => {...})`.\n- `r.w(x, y, z)` = buffer pixel of a world point. `r.W`, `r.H`, `r.ix`, `r.iy` (pass to dither functions), `r.view`, `r.visible(x, y, z)`.\n- X-ray: `r.actor(..., { xray: true })` shows the actor's hidden part, a translucent tint with a 1-px outline (a checker in `E.style.trans = 'dither'`), wherever a wall, a prop or shape at least chest high (22; `hides: true | false` says otherwise) or anything queued with `{ hides: true }` is drawn in front of it; not by one rising less than 8 above its feet (the roof it stands on, a cut-down wall). `xray: { color, outline (false: none), actors: true }`: with `actors` other bodies in front hide it too (a hero stays findable in a crowd). Afterimages: `ghost: { color, life, alpha, owner }`; `owner` (the actor's `{x, y, z}`) keeps a trail left nearer the camera sorted behind the live body.\n- Glowing things you draw yourself: `r.queue(x, y, z, fn, { emissive: true })` is drawn again after lighting so darkness never dims it (flames, lasers, magic, neon). It still hides behind whatever stands in front of it: walls and `r.actor` bodies do this on their own, and any other queued draw can join them with `{ solid: true }`. (Canvas lighting builds a depth-ordered mask for this; `game.r.glowMask = false` skips it on slow machines.)\n\n## Pixels, sprites, text, UI\n- `px.rect(g, x, y, w, h, c)`, `dot`, `line(g, x0, y0, x1, y1, c, width)`, `disc(g, x, y, r, c)`, `ell`, `poly(g, pts, c)`, `polyDither(g, pts, c, 0..1, r.ix, r.iy)`, `ddisc`, `sprite(g, spr, x, y, flip)` (or `{ flip, rot, scale }`), `glow(g, 0..1)` (GPU glow for what follows).\n- Sprites from strings (for coins, bullets, ships, icons, NES-style enemies): `const COIN = E.sprite(['.yy.', 'yYYy', '.yy.'], { y: '#f0b020', Y: '#fff0a0' }, scale)`. `.` and space are transparent. Frames: `const walk = E.anim([f1, f2, f3], 8, { pingpong })` (or one sheet: `E.anim(sheet, 8, { frames: 4 })`), drawn with `r.sprite(x, y, z, walk, { t })`. `r.sprite(..., { rot: angle, scale })` turns and scales in whole pixels (cached in 16 steps a turn; `steps: 32`); `E.recolor(spr, { '#3a8a4a': '#8a3a4a' })` swaps a palette.\n- Font (5x7 proportional with lower case; `font: 'tiny'` for 3x5): `E.font.text(g, str, x, y, color, { outline, shadow, gradient, scale, align, wrap, font })`, `E.font.title(g, str, x, y, { scale, colors, outline, depth, depthColor, align })`, `E.font.width(str, opts)`, `E.font.lineHeight(opts)`, `E.font.wrap(str, maxW, opts)`. Glyphs: ASCII plus `♥ ★ ← → ↑ ↓ • × ♪ ▸ ©`.\n- UI (inside `r.overlay(g => ...)`): `E.ui.box(g, x, y, w, h, { bg, border, gradient, alpha })` (a gradient window like Final Fantasy; `bg` can be `[top, bottom]`, `gradient: false` for flat, `alpha: .6` for a see-through HUD panel), `E.ui.bar(g, x, y, w, h, 0..1, color)`, `E.ui.hearts(g, x, y, hp, max)` (half hearts allowed), `rig.drawPortrait(g, x, y, size, { zoom: 3, turn: .6, bg, frame, cape: false, weapon: false, tint: '#808080' })` (character close-up; it stays upright while the rig kneels or lies down; `tint` greys out a KO'd hero).\n- Dialog: `const talk = new E.Dialog(game)`; `talk.say(['Line one.', 'Page two.'], { name: 'ELDER', portrait: elderRig, onDone, choices: ['YES', 'NO'], onChoice(i) })` (choices show on the last page: the keys, or a tap or click on one, pick); in update `if (talk.update(dt)) return;` (true while a modal box is open: play waits); in draw `talk.draw(r)`. `portrait` takes a Humanoid (live close-up; `portraitSize: 40`, `portraitOpts: { zoom, turn }`) or a function `(g, x, y, size) => {...}`. Radio chatter during play: `talk.say('Enemy fighters ahead!', { name, portrait, auto: 2.5, modal: false })` turns its pages and closes by itself while the game keeps running. Per line: `place: 'top' | 'middle' | 'bottom'`, `y` (pixels) and `alpha` (see-through box). A portrait's mouth moves while its text types. `new E.Dialog(game, { speed, lines, color, nameColor, voice, bg, border, vars })`; `talk.close()`, `talk.open`. Markup in the text: `{c=#ffd36a}gold{/c}` a color, `{w=.5}` a pause, `{s=12}slowly{/s}` letters a second, `{shake}..{/shake}`, `{wave}..{/wave}`, `{$name}` a value f","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"persistedOutputPath":"/home/micha/.claude/projects/-home-micha-dev-cc-matrix-runs-claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-build--20261103-184651--xa320fb-work/a73e9f9c-f8ef-4903-be97-67cc0d869215/tool-results/bxc9jmr45.txt","persistedOutputSize":42186},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01Nabc3pHoDpCT2XdSFCpN6r","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
