{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NJVGLsvbFPjywFR3Q9MTLf","type":"tool_result","content":"3:Genre: Pick the game genre(s) that best fit the source and would make the best game.\n5:Perspective: Pick the camera perspective(s) that best fit the genre and source and would make the best game.\n7:Game Title: A playful riff on SOURCE's name. Silently brainstorm 50 titles, then pick one with a real random number generator (e.g., run code).\n9:Research: Whether and how much to research is your choice.\n11:Package: For ease of deployment and sharing provide a single html file [GameTitle-SOURCE-claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-20261103-184651.html]. Dependenci\n13:Tech Stack: Build on https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/tree/main/dist/handoff. You may change anything in it, the engine included, and ad\n15:Art Direction: Use the engine’s native style as the base. Make new art assets for whatever makes the game feel like the source; the engine’s asset \n17:Animation: Use the engine’s native animation system as the base. Make new animations for whatever makes the game feel like the source.\n19:Audio: All music, sound effects and voices are written in code, with no audio files. Voices are Animalese/Simlish-style gibberish.\n21:Online: https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub\n26:Performance: Treat the performance budget as unlimited. Add as much detail, enemies, effects, lights, and shadows as the scene needs. Out of scope \n28:Testing: Test as much as you like with headless tools: scripts that play the game and check its state (the engine’s game.advance and game.debug, as\n32:## Goal\n38:Create a feature-complete demo. Capture what makes the source distinctive (its world, characters, themes and tone) and turn it into the mechanics, \n40:Include a dev mode, reachable from the pause menu, with the standard tools for testing the game quickly. Balance is key, so expose the most importa\n42:Attract Mode: After 10 seconds without input on the title/menu screen, start a looping AI-played demo or scripted replay (arcade/kiosk style); any \n44:Multiplayer: Follow https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub for multiplayer integration into ai-hub as well as for the game on i\n46:Narrative tone: Use a writing style similar to the source’s.\n48:## UI & UX: 10-Foot Controller-First Philosophy\n49:Design the game around a PS5 DualSense (or any pad the browser reports in its standard mapping) played on a TV at 10 feet. With keyboard and mouse \n51:Do not use overly dense, web-style data screens.\n53:Touch-only devices: no touch controls; show \"Controller or keyboard required\" and the settings button. Let the attract mode run.\n55:## Done\n58:## Source Information\n95:Testing Feedback\n00-core.js 11-data-about.js 20-track.js 30-sim.js 50-audio.js 55-glyphs.js 60-render.js 70-hud.js 75-ui.js 80-director.js 85-dev.js 90-hub.js 95-main.js manifest.json","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a73e9f9c-f8ef-4903-be97-67cc0d869215","uuid":"fad4d02f-7f67-4c92-b151-53e69d5ddd1c","timestamp":"2026-11-04T02:35:03.792Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"3:Genre: Pick the game genre(s) that best fit the source and would make the best game.\n5:Perspective: Pick the camera perspective(s) that best fit the genre and source and would make the best game.\n7:Game Title: A playful riff on SOURCE's name. Silently brainstorm 50 titles, then pick one with a real random number generator (e.g., run code).\n9:Research: Whether and how much to research is your choice.\n11:Package: For ease of deployment and sharing provide a single html file [GameTitle-SOURCE-claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-20261103-184651.html]. Dependenci\n13:Tech Stack: Build on https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/tree/main/dist/handoff. You may change anything in it, the engine included, and ad\n15:Art Direction: Use the engine’s native style as the base. Make new art assets for whatever makes the game feel like the source; the engine’s asset \n17:Animation: Use the engine’s native animation system as the base. Make new animations for whatever makes the game feel like the source.\n19:Audio: All music, sound effects and voices are written in code, with no audio files. Voices are Animalese/Simlish-style gibberish.\n21:Online: https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub\n26:Performance: Treat the performance budget as unlimited. Add as much detail, enemies, effects, lights, and shadows as the scene needs. Out of scope \n28:Testing: Test as much as you like with headless tools: scripts that play the game and check its state (the engine’s game.advance and game.debug, as\n32:## Goal\n38:Create a feature-complete demo. Capture what makes the source distinctive (its world, characters, themes and tone) and turn it into the mechanics, \n40:Include a dev mode, reachable from the pause menu, with the standard tools for testing the game quickly. Balance is key, so expose the most importa\n42:Attract Mode: After 10 seconds without input on the title/menu screen, start a looping AI-played demo or scripted replay (arcade/kiosk style); any \n44:Multiplayer: Follow https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub for multiplayer integration into ai-hub as well as for the game on i\n46:Narrative tone: Use a writing style similar to the source’s.\n48:## UI & UX: 10-Foot Controller-First Philosophy\n49:Design the game around a PS5 DualSense (or any pad the browser reports in its standard mapping) played on a TV at 10 feet. With keyboard and mouse \n51:Do not use overly dense, web-style data screens.\n53:Touch-only devices: no touch controls; show \"Controller or keyboard required\" and the settings button. Let the attract mode run.\n55:## Done\n58:## Source Information\n95:Testing Feedback\n00-core.js 11-data-about.js 20-track.js 30-sim.js 50-audio.js 55-glyphs.js 60-render.js 70-hud.js 75-ui.js 80-director.js 85-dev.js 90-hub.js 95-main.js manifest.json","stderr":"","interrup