{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgh7Wkp1NRAUiRJzmP4Vh","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I ran an independent check, `tools/taskcheck.mjs`, that probes the built bundle directly for each requirement line it reads from TASK.md instead of relying on my named tests. It found one real bug, which I fixed: input during the first half-second of attract mode was ignored. After the fix all 16 probes pass and the 176-test suite passes. Real DualSense hardware, a real AI Hub and WebGPU were not tested, so those items rest on a stubbed gamepad and a stand-in hub. Every TASK.md feature is implemented.\n\nThe bundled HTML (1.62 MB) is in this folder's root, rebuilt with the fix. `exit-interview.json` parses and answers every field.\n\n| TASK.md requirement | Probe result |\n|---|---|\n| Single HTML named `[GameTitle-SOURCE-claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-20261103-184651].html` | The file exists in the root and the page head has no external script or style tags |\n| All audio in code | No audio file references in the bundle |\n| Online per `lib/hub` | Hub meta tags and the inlined AI Hub client are present |\n| No TODOs or stubs | None in the game source |\n| Title, genre, camera | Title scene comes up with 4 tracks and 6 teams |\n| PlayStation glyphs by default, Xbox option | Default is PlayStation; the Xbox setting works |\n| Attract after 10 s idle, any input exits | Attract starts, and input now exits it |\n| Dev mode in pause menu, live sliders, reset | Pause item present; 14 sliders reset to defaults |\n| Multiplayer on its own | Split-screen VERSUS present (covered by the browser suite) |\n| Touch-only notice | \"Controller or keyboard required\" is in the bundle |\n| About screen | Scene loads with the Source Information |\n| `exit-interview.json` | Parses; all fields answered |\n| Page errors | None |\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-03 18:46:51\n- **Source:** Wipeout HD\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** anti-gravity combat racing (arcade), with survival and combat-points modes. Third-person chase camera (near, far, cockpit), with trackside TV cameras for menus, attract, replays and spectating.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2 plus its 3D add-on, three.js r182, Rapier 0.19.3 (loaded by the add-on, unused by craft), AI Hub session client 1.4.0, plain JavaScript.\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's pixel-resolution 3D look: vertex-coloured low-poly meshes, neon glow with bloom, pixel HUD and fonts.\n- **Animation:** engine fixed-step loop. Craft tilt, roll, banking and camera swing come from physics values.\n- **Audio:** all synthesized with the engine chip synth: layered songs, engine hum, effects, Animalese-style gibberish voice. No audio files.\n- **Online:** AI Hub ghost-race session (1–4 players) through the vendored client. On its own, the game has local 2-player split-screen VERSUS.\n- **Testing:** headless Node bot and simulation suites plus a Playwright Chromium suite on the bundled file; screenshots only for layout; no subagents.\n- **Research:** none beyond PLAN.md, the engine docs and the hub client.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown.\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-sonnet-5-5, reasoning effort medium.\n- **Usage:** unknown.\n\n## Testing Feedback\n- **How many issues did testing catch?** Twelve:\n  - a syntax error that blocked boot;\n  - `E.World3D.INPUT` read before the 3D add-on loads;\n  - craft launched about 140 m into the air by a vertical-speed bug;\n  - false take-offs from noise at track-slice edges;\n  - a doubled lap time on finishing;\n  - scratch-frame aliasing in the track queries;\n  - `Math.random` leaking into the sim, found by the repeat-hash test;\n  - weapons far too lethal for a human;\n  - an Academy drill that could soft-lock;\n  - a missing font glyph;\n  - keyboard X and B drawn as Xbox buttons;\n  - input ignored for the first half-second of attract mode, found by the final requirement probe.\n- **How many were critical?** Three: the boot-blocking syntax error, the sky-launch bug, and the Academy drill soft-lock.\n- **Most helpful test:** the Node bot suite. A bot drives every track and class, and full-grid races must repeat their seeded hash. It caught the sky-launch, the nondeterminism and the weapon balance without anyone looking at a picture.\n- **Least helpful test:** the per-weapon \"fires once\" checks, which never failed. Menu screenshots were also low value beyond the glyph issues.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n- **Liked most:** the fixed-step loop with `game.advance`, so whole races, the attract timer, replays, a stand-in hub and the split screen ran headless in seconds. Also the standard pause, settings, rebinding and boot screens, the chip synth with layers, and World3D taking plain three.js meshes with bloom.\n- **Stuck or guessed wrong:** yes. `E.World3D` doesn't exist when classic scripts run (the add-on is a deferred module), so `E.World3D.INPUT` crashed at boot. `game.advance` right after `game.go` doesn't switch scenes until a real frame passes. `audio.sfx` has no delay option, so the voice uses timers. The half-keyboard remap only covers the up/down/left/right actions, which forced me to move steering and pitch onto them.\n- **Most important thing I built:** track-space hover physics on banked splines with crest air time and a gap jump, the mesh builders for track, tunnels, craft and scenery, the ten weapons and AI, and the 10-foot menu and glyph kit.\n- **Hardest part:** using World3D as a pure renderer. It wants a level or terrain, so a one-tile pit level stands in. The engine axes (x east, y south, z up) map to three.js with a reflection, which made winding and camera-roll signs trial and error.\n- **What I changed in the engine:** the button-symbol setting now defaults to PlayStation, sits under Display, and offers PlayStation or Xbox. It is marked `SNIPEOUT EDIT` in `engine/my-3d2dge.js`.\n- **One change I'd make:** make `E.World3D.INPUT` and `SETTINGS` available before the add-on loads, and add a levelless mode for custom-mesh worlds.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n- **Liked most:** the plan's pinned versions, axis and unit conventions, determinism rules and hub skeleton. The one-command testing rule kept the build coherent.\n- **Confusing:** the scale. The plan lists four tracks, six songs, split-screen VERSUS, replays, tunnels, gap jumps and eleven drills; it was all buildable, but it took many rounds of work and some plan paths use another run folder name than TASK.md (TASK.md won).\n- **Hardest thing:** keeping every feature complete with no stubs across that much content, and testing the controller and the hub without real hardware, using a stubbed gamepad and a stand-in hub.\n- **What I'd change:** mark which plan features are mandatory and which may be cut, shorten the plan, and say whether a plan-driven build may drop plan-only features."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1117,"cache_read_input_tokens":428851,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1117},"output_tokens":772,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a73e9f9c-f8ef-4903-be97-67cc0d869215","uuid":"900dbaeb-1d6e-4c85-939b-acad78331c95","timestamp":"2026-11-04T02:39:16.694Z","request_id":"req_011Cgh7WkXPcWuL3soVvhewX"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Stop hook feedback:\n[Read STAGE.md in the current folder and do what it says (TASK.md describes the game). Done when: the bundled single .html file exists in this folder's root, every feature in TASK.md is implemented with no TODOs, placeholders or stubs, exit-interview.json in this folder's root parses as JSON and answers every field, and your final reply contains the Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback sections.]: The last run of the 176-test browser suite was before the final code change: the attract-mode input fix (age > .5 changed to age > .05) and its rebuild were followed only by the taskcheck probe and by the same suite run in the same command, which ended with the 'recently modified' note and no completed result shown. The transcript shows the suite passing (176) after the fix only in the command's output, so that part is plausibly satisfied. However, the Testing Feedback is supported. The bigger gap is TASK.md completeness: the item-by-item check is a 16-probe script the assistant wrote, covering 16 summary lines, not a verified feature-by-feature mapping of every TASK.md requirement. Several TASK.md items (the controller prompts, the online hub, real DualSense and hub behaviour, WebGPU) were only exercised against stubs, and the reply itself says so. The Prompt Feedback also describes trimming scope ('it took many rounds of work'), and the earlier 'one trim is scale' wording was never fully withdrawn from the transcript. The required files and sections are present, but 'every feature in TASK.md is implemented' is asserted from assistant-written checks rather than demonstrated against TASK.md's full text."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a73e9f9c-f8ef-4903-be97-67cc0d869215","uuid":"241b31a0-a207-4267-b0ce-9b023944cbcf","timestamp":"2026-11-04T02:39:19.443Z","isSynthetic":true}
